    content: `
<p>The nervous system controls movement, sensation, speech, vision, memory, balance, coordination, and consciousness. It includes the brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves. Problems affecting these areas can produce symptoms ranging from mild discomfort to serious medical emergencies.</p>
<p>Headaches may be related to migraine, numbness may result from nerve problems, and tremors can occur with several neurological conditions. Sudden weakness, facial drooping, or speech difficulty may indicate a stroke and requires immediate emergency care.</p>
<p>Because neurological symptoms can have many possible causes, knowing when to seek professional evaluation is important. Dr. Jasti Shivani, MD (General Medicine), DM (Neurology), Consultant Neurophysician, provides neurological consultation for patients experiencing headaches, seizures, dizziness, numbness, weakness, tremors, memory problems, movement disorders, and other neurological concerns.</p>
<h3>What Does a Neurologist Treat?</h3>
<p>A neurologist or neurophysician evaluates disorders affecting the brain, spinal cord, peripheral nerves, and neuromuscular system. Common conditions include migraine, epilepsy, stroke, Parkinson’s disease, neuropathy, movement disorders, vertigo, memory disorders, and other neurological conditions.</p>
<h3>Symptoms That May Need Neurological Evaluation</h3>
<h3>Persistent or Recurrent Headaches</h3>
<p>Frequent, worsening, or changing headaches may require evaluation, particularly when they interfere with daily activities or occur with vomiting, visual disturbances, or other neurological symptoms. A sudden, extremely severe headache reaching maximum intensity rapidly requires urgent medical assessment.</p>
<h3>Migraine</h3>
<p>Migraine can cause throbbing headache, nausea, vomiting, sensitivity to light or sound, visual aura, dizziness, and difficulty concentrating. Frequent attacks or headaches that interfere with daily life may benefit from neurological assessment and an individualized treatment plan.</p>
<h3>Numbness and Tingling</h3>
<p>Occasional tingling may occur from temporary nerve pressure. Persistent or recurrent numbness, however, can be associated with peripheral neuropathy, diabetes-related nerve damage, nerve compression, vitamin deficiencies, spine-related problems, autoimmune conditions, or other neurological disorders. Sudden one-sided numbness is particularly concerning because it can indicate stroke.</p>
<h3>Unexplained Weakness</h3>
<p>Weakness may affect one limb, one side of the body, or multiple limbs. Neurological causes include stroke, nerve disorders, muscle disorders, spinal conditions, and other diseases. Sudden weakness accompanied by facial drooping or speech difficulty is a medical emergency.</p>
<h3>Dizziness and Vertigo</h3>
<p>Vertigo describes a sensation that you or your surroundings are moving or spinning. While some causes involve the inner ear, others may involve the nervous system. Urgent evaluation is important when severe dizziness occurs with difficulty walking, double vision, weakness, numbness, speech problems, severe headache, or loss of coordination.</p>
<h3>Seizures and Loss of Awareness</h3>
<p>Not all seizures involve dramatic shaking. Some may cause staring, loss of awareness, repetitive movements, unusual sensations, or temporary confusion. A first suspected seizure or repeated unexplained episodes of altered awareness should receive neurological evaluation.</p>
<h3>Tremors</h3>
<p>Tremors are involuntary rhythmic movements and may occur with essential tremor, Parkinson’s disease, medication effects, thyroid abnormalities, anxiety, metabolic problems, or other conditions. Doctors assess whether tremor occurs at rest, during movement, or while performing specific activities.</p>
<h3>Memory and Balance Problems</h3>
<p>Persistent memory changes that interfere with daily life, including repeated questions, getting lost, difficulty managing familiar tasks, or significant personality changes, should be evaluated. Causes can include neurological disorders, sleep problems, medications, nutritional deficiencies, mood disorders, and other medical conditions.</p>
<p>Walking difficulties, balance problems, shuffling, or repeated falls may also indicate Parkinson’s disease, stroke, neuropathy, cerebellar disorders, movement disorders, or spinal conditions.</p>
<h3>Sudden Neurological Symptoms: Recognize Stroke</h3>
<h3>Remember FAST:</h3>
<p>* F – Face: One side may droop.</p>
<p>* A – Arms: One arm may become weak or numb.</p>
<p>* S – Speech: Speech may become slurred or difficult to understand.</p>
<p>* T – Time: Seek emergency medical care immediately.</p>
<p>Sudden vision changes, severe headache, confusion, seizures, or loss of balance can also accompany serious neurological emergencies.</p>
<h3>What Happens During a Neurological Consultation?</h3>
<p>A consultation generally includes discussion of symptom onset, duration, frequency, associated symptoms, medical history, medications, and family history. Examination may assess speech, eye movements, strength, reflexes, sensation, coordination, balance, and walking.</p>
<p>Depending on findings, investigations such as blood tests, CT, MRI, EEG, nerve conduction studies, or electromyography may be recommended. Not every patient requires every test.</p>
<h3>Neurological Care With Dr. Jasti Shivani</h3>
<p>Dr. Jasti Shivani, MD (General Medicine), DM (Neurology), Consultant Neurophysician, provides neurological consultation and management for headaches, migraine, seizures, dizziness, tremors, numbness, weakness, memory concerns, movement difficulties, and other neurological symptoms. A comprehensive assessment can help identify the likely cause and guide appropriate treatment, investigations, rehabilitation, or specialist referral.</p>
<h3>When Should You Seek Emergency Care?</h3>
<p>Seek immediate medical attention for sudden facial drooping, weakness, numbness, speech difficulty, vision loss, severe sudden headache, loss of consciousness, prolonged or repeated seizures, severe balance problems, or new severe confusion.</p>
<h3>Conclusion</h3>
<p>Persistent, recurrent, worsening, or unexplained neurological symptoms should not be ignored. Early neurological assessment can help identify underlying causes, establish an accurate diagnosis, begin appropriate treatment, and reduce complications. If you experience concerning neurological symptoms.    `
