    slug: 'diabetes-causes-symptoms-types-diagnosis-and-treat',
    title: `Diabetes: Causes, Symptoms, Types, Diagnosis and Treatment`,
    excerpt: `Diabetes mellitus, commonly referred to as diabetes, is a chronic medical condition that affects how the body turns food into energy. Normally, your body breaks down most of the food you eat into sugar (glucose) and releases it into your bloodstream.`,
    category: 'General Medicine',
    date: '109 Sep 2026',
    readTime: '4 min read',
    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/garath-ganesh-blog-1.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Diabetes mellitus, commonly referred to as diabetes, is a chronic medical condition that affects how the body turns food into energy. Normally, your body breaks down most of the food you eat into sugar (glucose) and releases it into your bloodstream.</p>\n<p>When your blood sugar goes up, it signals your pancreas to release insulin, a hormone that acts like a key to let the blood sugar into your body's cells for use as energy.</p>\n<p>In diabetes, your body either doesn't make enough insulin or can't use the insulin it makes as well as it should. Over time, high blood sugar can cause serious health problems.</p>\n<p>Patients who need comprehensive diabetes care can consult Dr. Garath Ganesh, MBBS, DNB (General Medicine), Consultant General Physician & Diabetologist, an in-house consultant providing evaluation and management for patients with diabetes and other general medical conditions.</p>\n<h3>Types of Diabetes</h3>\n<h3>Type 1 Diabetes</h3>\n<p>Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune condition where the body's immune system attacks and destroys the insulin-producing cells in the pancreas. People with type 1 diabetes produce little or no insulin and require daily insulin administration to survive. This type often develops in childhood or adolescence but can appear at any age.</p>\n<h3>Type 2 Diabetes</h3>\n<p>Type 2 diabetes is the most common form, accounting for the vast majority of cases. It occurs when the body becomes resistant to insulin or doesn't make enough of it. It is strongly linked to lifestyle factors, body weight, and age, although genetic factors also play a role.</p>\n<h3>Gestational Diabetes</h3>\n<p>This type develops in some pregnant women and usually goes away after the baby is born. However, having gestational diabetes increases the risk of developing type 2 diabetes later in life.</p>\n<h3>Common Symptoms of Diabetes</h3>\n<h3>The symptoms of diabetes can vary depending on blood sugar levels. Common signs include:</h3>\n<h3>* Frequent urination</h3>\n<h3>* Excessive thirst</h3>\n<h3>* Unexplained weight loss</h3>\n<h3>* Extreme hunger</h3>\n<h3>* Blurry vision</h3>\n<h3>* Feeling very tired</h3>\n<h3>* Slow-healing sores</h3>\n<h3>* Frequent infections, such as skin or gum infections</h3>\n<p>In type 2 diabetes, symptoms can develop slowly over years and may go unnoticed. Some people find out they have the disease only when they experience diabetes-related health problems.</p>\n<h3>Risk Factors</h3>\n<h3>For Type 2 Diabetes:</h3>\n<h3>* Being overweight or obese</h3>\n<h3>* Lack of physical activity</h3>\n<h3>* Family history of diabetes</h3>\n<h3>* Age (risk increases as you get older)</h3>\n<h3>* Previous history of gestational diabetes</h3>\n<h3>* High blood pressure or abnormal cholesterol levels</h3>\n<h3>Complications of Uncontrolled Diabetes</h3>\n<h3>Over time, high blood sugar levels can damage various organs and tissues in the body. Potential complications include:</h3>\n<h3>* Cardiovascular disease: Increased risk of heart attack and stroke.</h3>\n<h3>* Neuropathy: Nerve damage leading to tingling, numbness, or pain, often starting in the feet.</h3>\n<h3>* Nephropathy: Kidney damage that can lead to kidney failure.</h3>\n<h3>* Retinopathy: Eye damage that can lead to vision loss or blindness.</h3>\n<h3>* Foot problems: Poor blood flow and nerve damage increase the risk of serious foot infections.</h3>\n<h3>How Is Diabetes Diagnosed?</h3>\n<h3>Diabetes is diagnosed using blood tests. Common tests include:</h3>\n<h3>* Fasting blood sugar test: Measures blood glucose after an overnight fast.</h3>\n<h3>* HbA1c test: Indicates average blood sugar levels over the past two to three months.</h3>\n<h3>* Oral glucose tolerance test: Measures blood sugar before and after drinking a sweet liquid.</h3>\n<h3>* Random blood sugar test: Can be performed at any time, regardless of when you last ate.</h3>\n<h3>Treatment and Management</h3>\n<p>The goal of diabetes treatment is to keep blood sugar levels within a target range to prevent complications.</p>\n<h3>Management involves:</h3>\n<h3>1. Lifestyle Modifications</h3>\n<h3>* Healthy Eating: Focusing on a balanced diet rich in vegetables, fruits, whole grains, and lean proteins while monitoring carbohydrate intake.</h3>\n<h3>* Regular Exercise: Physical activity helps the body use insulin better. Aim for moderate activity like brisk walking.</h3>\n<h3>* Weight Management: Losing even a small amount of weight can improve blood sugar control in people with type 2 diabetes.</h3>\n<h3>2. Medication</h3>\n<h3>Depending on the type and severity of diabetes, treatment may involve:</h3>\n<h3>* Oral medications (such as Metformin) that help lower blood sugar in various ways.</h3>\n<h3>* Insulin therapy, which is required for all type 1 patients and some type 2 patients.</h3>\n<h3>* Other injectable medicines that help the body manage glucose.</h3>\n<h3>3. Monitoring</h3>\n<p>Regular checking of blood sugar levels helps you and your doctor understand how food, activity, and medications affect your glucose control.</p>\n<h3>General Medical Consultation With Dr. Garath Ganesh</h3>\n<p>Dr. Garath Ganesh, MBBS, DNB (General Medicine), Consultant General Physician & Diabetologist, provides comprehensive evaluation and management for patients with diabetes.</p>\n<p>As an in-house consultant, Dr. Garath Ganesh can assess blood sugar control, review risk factors, monitor for potential complications, and guide patients toward individualized treatment plans that may include medication management and lifestyle advice.</p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Diabetes is a lifelong condition that requires consistent management. With appropriate medical care, healthy lifestyle choices, and regular monitoring, most people with diabetes can lead full and active lives while reducing their risk of serious health complications.</p>\n<p>If you have risk factors for diabetes, experience symptoms, or need help managing your current blood sugar levels, consult Dr. Garath Ganesh, MBBS, DNB (General Medicine), Consultant General Physician & Diabetologist, for professional medical guidance.</p>\n`
