    content: `
<p>The thyroid is a small butterfly-shaped gland located at the front of the neck, but it plays an important role in regulating metabolism, energy use, heart rate, temperature, and many other body functions.</p>\n<p>When the thyroid produces too much or too little hormone, a person can develop symptoms affecting multiple systems. Thyroid disorders are common and can occur at any age.</p>\n<p>Some people may experience tiredness, weight changes, mood changes, changes in heart rate, hair loss, or difficulty sleeping without realizing that the thyroid may be involved.</p>\n<p>Appropriate blood tests and medical evaluation can help identify thyroid disorders and guide treatment.</p>\n<p>Patients with symptoms suggestive of thyroid problems can consult Dr. Garath Ganesh, MBBS, DNB (General Medicine), Consultant General Physician & Diabetologist, an in-house consultant providing comprehensive general medical care.</p>\n<h3>What Is the Thyroid Gland?</h3>\n<p>The thyroid is an endocrine gland located in the neck.</p>\n<h3>It produces thyroid hormones that influence:</h3>\n<h3>* Metabolism</h3>\n<h3>* Heart rate</h3>\n<h3>* Body temperature</h3>\n<h3>* Energy levels</h3>\n<h3>* Digestion</h3>\n<h3>* Muscle function</h3>\n<h3>* Brain function</h3>\n<h3>* Growth and development</h3>\n<p>The main thyroid hormones include thyroxine and triiodothyronine.</p>\n<p>Their production is regulated by thyroid-stimulating hormone, or TSH, which is produced by the pituitary gland.</p>\n<h3>What Are Thyroid Disorders?</h3>\n<p>Thyroid disorders occur when the gland produces too much or too little thyroid hormone or develops structural abnormalities.</p>\n<h3>Common thyroid conditions include:</h3>\n<h3>* Hypothyroidism</h3>\n<h3>* Hyperthyroidism</h3>\n<h3>* Thyroid nodules</h3>\n<h3>* Goiter</h3>\n<h3>* Thyroiditis</h3>\n<h3>* Thyroid cancer</h3>\n<h3>Hypothyroidism</h3>\n<p>Hypothyroidism occurs when the thyroid does not produce enough thyroid hormone.</p>\n<h3>Common symptoms include:</h3>\n<h3>* Fatigue</h3>\n<h3>* Weight gain</h3>\n<h3>* Feeling cold</h3>\n<h3>* Dry skin</h3>\n<h3>* Constipation</h3>\n<h3>* Hair loss</h3>\n<h3>* Slow heart rate</h3>\n<h3>* Muscle aches</h3>\n<h3>* Difficulty concentrating</h3>\n<h3>* Depression or low mood</h3>\n<h3>* Menstrual changes</h3>\n<p>Symptoms can develop gradually and may be mistaken for stress or normal ageing.</p>\n<h3>Causes of Hypothyroidism</h3>\n<p>One common cause is autoimmune thyroid disease, such as Hashimoto's thyroiditis.</p>\n<h3>Other causes may include:</h3>\n<h3>* Certain medications</h3>\n<h3>* Previous thyroid surgery</h3>\n<h3>* Radiation treatment</h3>\n<h3>* Iodine-related problems</h3>\n<h3>* Pituitary disorders</h3>\n<h3>* Certain congenital conditions</h3>\n<h3>Hyperthyroidism</h3>\n<p>Hyperthyroidism occurs when the thyroid produces excessive amounts of thyroid hormones.</p>\n<h3>Symptoms may include:</h3>\n<h3>* Unintentional weight loss</h3>\n<h3>* Increased appetite</h3>\n<h3>* Rapid heartbeat</h3>\n<h3>* Anxiety</h3>\n<h3>* Tremors</h3>\n<h3>* Excessive sweating</h3>\n<h3>* Heat intolerance</h3>\n<h3>* Difficulty sleeping</h3>\n<h3>* Frequent bowel movements</h3>\n<h3>* Muscle weakness</h3>\n<p>Some patients may experience irregular heart rhythms.</p>\n<h3>Causes of Hyperthyroidism</h3>\n<h3>Common causes include:</h3>\n<h3>* Graves' disease</h3>\n<h3>* Toxic thyroid nodules</h3>\n<h3>* Thyroid inflammation</h3>\n<h3>* Excessive thyroid hormone medication</h3>\n<p>The underlying cause must be identified because treatment differs.</p>\n<h3>Thyroid Nodules</h3>\n<p>A thyroid nodule is a lump within the thyroid gland.</p>\n<p>Most thyroid nodules are not cancerous, but some require further evaluation.</p>\n<h3>A doctor may recommend:</h3>\n<h3>* Thyroid ultrasound</h3>\n<h3>* Blood tests</h3>\n<h3>* Fine-needle aspiration in selected cases</h3>\n<h3>* Additional imaging or specialist evaluation</h3>\n<p>A rapidly growing neck lump, difficulty swallowing, voice changes, or breathing difficulty should be medically evaluated.</p>\n<h3>Goiter</h3>\n<p>A goiter refers to enlargement of the thyroid gland.</p>\n<h3>It can occur because of:</h3>\n<h3>* Iodine deficiency</h3>\n<h3>* Autoimmune thyroid disease</h3>\n<h3>* Thyroid nodules</h3>\n<h3>* Other thyroid disorders</h3>\n<p>A goiter may be visible as swelling in the neck or may be detected during examination or imaging.</p>\n<h3>How Are Thyroid Disorders Diagnosed?</h3>\n<p>Blood tests are commonly used.</p>\n<h3>TSH</h3>\n<p>TSH is usually one of the initial tests used to evaluate thyroid function.</p>\n<h3>Free T4</h3>\n<p>Free thyroxine may provide additional information about thyroid hormone levels.</p>\n<h3>Other Tests</h3>\n<h3>Depending on the clinical situation, doctors may recommend:</h3>\n<h3>* Thyroid antibodies</h3>\n<h3>* Thyroid ultrasound</h3>\n<h3>* Additional hormone tests</h3>\n<h3>* Fine-needle aspiration of selected nodules</h3>\n<p>The appropriate tests depend on symptoms and examination findings.</p>\n<h3>Thyroid Disorders and Weight</h3>\n<p>Weight changes can occur with thyroid disorders, but thyroid disease is not the only cause of weight gain or weight loss.</p>\n<p>Hypothyroidism can contribute to weight gain, while hyperthyroidism may cause weight loss.</p>\n<p>However, significant weight changes should be evaluated rather than automatically attributed to the thyroid.</p>\n<h3>Thyroid Disorders and Diabetes</h3>\n<p>Thyroid disorders and diabetes can occur together, particularly because some autoimmune conditions can overlap.</p>\n<p>Patients with diabetes who develop symptoms suggestive of thyroid dysfunction should discuss testing with their doctor when appropriate.</p>\n<h3>Thyroid Problems During Pregnancy</h3>\n<p>Thyroid hormones are important during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Untreated thyroid disorders can affect both mother and baby, so thyroid function may need to be monitored in women at increased risk or when symptoms suggest thyroid disease.</p>\n<p>Treatment during pregnancy should always be managed by a qualified healthcare professional.</p>\n<h3>Treatment of Hypothyroidism</h3>\n<p>Hypothyroidism is commonly treated with thyroid hormone replacement.</p>\n<h3>The medication dose depends on:</h3>\n<h3>* Age</h3>\n<h3>* Weight</h3>\n<h3>* Severity of thyroid dysfunction</h3>\n<h3>* Pregnancy status</h3>\n<h3>* Other medical conditions</h3>\n<h3>* Blood test results</h3>\n<p>Regular follow-up blood tests help determine whether the dose needs adjustment.</p>\n<p>Patients should take thyroid medication according to medical instructions.</p>\n<h3>Treatment of Hyperthyroidism</h3>\n<h3>Treatment depends on the underlying cause and may include:</h3>\n<h3>* Medicines</h3>\n<h3>* Radioactive iodine in selected cases</h3>\n<h3>* Surgery in selected cases</h3>\n<h3>* Management of symptoms such as rapid heartbeat</h3>\n<p>The appropriate option depends on the patient's condition and medical history.</p>\n<h3>Lifestyle and Thyroid Health</h3>\n<p>A balanced diet and healthy lifestyle support general health, but patients should avoid unnecessary high-dose supplements or extreme diets marketed as "thyroid cures."</p>\n<p>Iodine is essential for thyroid hormone production, but excessive iodine can also cause thyroid problems in some individuals.</p>\n<p>Supplements should be taken only when medically appropriate.</p>\n<h3>When Should You See a Doctor?</h3>\n<h3>Consider medical evaluation if you experience:</h3>\n<h3>* Persistent fatigue</h3>\n<h3>* Unexplained weight changes</h3>\n<h3>* Persistent constipation</h3>\n<h3>* Heat or cold intolerance</h3>\n<h3>* Rapid or irregular heartbeat</h3>\n<h3>* Tremors</h3>\n<h3>* Hair loss</h3>\n<h3>* Neck swelling</h3>\n<h3>* Difficulty swallowing</h3>\n<h3>* Menstrual changes</h3>\n<h3>* Persistent mood changes</h3>\n<h3>General Medical Consultation With Dr. Garath Ganesh</h3>\n<p>Dr. Garath Ganesh, MBBS, DNB (General Medicine), Consultant General Physician & Diabetologist, provides evaluation and management for thyroid-related symptoms and a wide range of general medical conditions.</p>\n<p>As an in-house consultant, Dr. Garath Ganesh can assess symptoms, review relevant blood tests, identify associated health conditions, and guide patients toward appropriate treatment or specialist referral when required.</p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Thyroid disorders are common and can affect many aspects of health. Symptoms such as unexplained fatigue, weight changes, palpitations, tremors, constipation, hair loss, or neck swelling should not automatically be attributed to lifestyle or stress.</p>\n<p>Simple blood tests can often help identify thyroid dysfunction, while additional investigations may be required in selected cases.</p>\n<p>Early diagnosis and appropriate treatment can help restore thyroid hormone balance and reduce symptoms.</p>\n<p>If you have persistent symptoms or concerns about your thyroid function, consult Dr. Garath Ganesh, MBBS, DNB (General Medicine), Consultant General Physician & Diabetologist, for appropriate medical evaluation and individualized guidance.</p>\n`
