    readTime: '4 min read',
    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/jagadeesh-mavilla-blog-1.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Knee pain is a common problem that can affect people of different age groups. It may appear after an injury, develop gradually with age, or occur because of excessive physical activity. While occasional discomfort may improve with rest, persistent or worsening knee pain should not be ignored.</p>\n<p>The knee is one of the body's major weight-bearing joints. It connects the thigh bone to the shin bone and depends on muscles, ligaments, tendons, cartilage, and other structures for stability and movement. Problems affecting any of these structures can result in pain, stiffness, swelling, or difficulty walking.</p>\n<p>One of the common causes of knee pain is osteoarthritis. It develops when the protective cartilage covering the joint gradually wears down. People may experience stiffness after sitting for a long time, discomfort while climbing stairs, or pain during walking. As the condition progresses, everyday activities can become increasingly difficult.</p>\n<p>Sports injuries are another frequent cause. Activities involving running, jumping, sudden turns, or physical contact can put significant stress on the knee. Ligament injuries, meniscus tears, tendon problems, and cartilage injuries may occur following sports-related trauma. Some injuries cause immediate pain and swelling, while others may initially seem manageable but become troublesome later.</p>\n<p>Knee pain can also result from accidents or falls. A sudden impact can damage bones, ligaments, or soft tissues around the joint. In such situations, attempting to continue normal activities without proper assessment may worsen the injury.</p>\n<p>Certain symptoms indicate that professional evaluation may be necessary. Severe or persistent pain, significant swelling, instability, difficulty bearing weight, restricted movement, or repeated episodes of the knee giving way should receive medical attention. A detailed clinical examination helps determine the possible source of the problem.</p>\n<p>Orthopedic evaluation may include physical examination and, when required, imaging such as X-rays or MRI. The treatment depends on the diagnosis, severity, age, activity level, and overall condition of the patient. Mild problems may respond to activity modification, physiotherapy, medicines, and exercises. More significant injuries may require advanced orthopedic procedures.</p>\n<p>Modern orthopedic care includes minimally invasive techniques for selected conditions. Arthroscopy, for example, allows surgeons to examine and treat certain problems through small incisions. For patients with advanced joint damage, knee replacement may be considered when pain and loss of function significantly affect daily life.</p>\n<p>At Srikara Hospitals, Lakdikapul, Dr. Jagadeesh Mavilla is a Consultant Robotic Joint Replacement Surgeon with expertise in arthroscopy, sports medicine, and complex trauma. The orthopedic team can assess knee-related problems and help determine an appropriate treatment pathway.</p>\n<p>Taking care of knee health also involves maintaining a healthy body weight, strengthening the muscles around the joint, using proper techniques during exercise, and allowing sufficient recovery after physical activity. People involved in sports should also pay attention to warm-up, flexibility, footwear, and injury-prevention practices.</p>\n<p>Knee pain does not always mean that surgery is required. Early assessment can help identify the underlying problem and provide an opportunity to manage it appropriately. If symptoms continue or interfere with normal activities, consulting an orthopedic specialist can be an important step toward restoring mobility and maintaining joint health.</p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Knee pain can have many causes, ranging from temporary strain to significant joint or ligament problems. Understanding the symptoms and seeking timely orthopedic assessment can help prevent minor problems from becoming long-term limitations. Appropriate diagnosis and individualized treatment can support better movement, comfort, and quality of life.</p>\n
    `
