    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/jagadeesh-mavilla-blog-5.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Knee arthritis can gradually change the way a person walks, exercises, works, and performs everyday activities. In the early stages, symptoms may be manageable with non-surgical treatment. However, advanced arthritis can cause persistent pain and stiffness that significantly affect quality of life.</p>\n<p>The knee joint contains a layer of cartilage that allows the bones to move smoothly against each other. With osteoarthritis, this protective cartilage gradually deteriorates. As the condition progresses, the joint may become painful and stiff, particularly during activities that place weight on the knee.</p>\n<p>Common symptoms include pain while walking, climbing stairs, standing for long periods, or getting up after sitting. Some people may also experience swelling, reduced movement, grinding sensations, or increasing difficulty performing routine activities.</p>\n<p>Treatment usually depends on the severity of the condition. Doctors may initially recommend measures such as weight management, appropriate exercises, physiotherapy, activity modification, and medicines when clinically suitable. These approaches can help many people manage symptoms and maintain function.</p>\n<p>However, advanced arthritis may continue to interfere with daily life despite conservative treatment. When pain becomes persistent and significantly restricts mobility, an orthopedic specialist may discuss surgical options, including knee replacement.</p>\n<p>Knee replacement involves removing damaged portions of the joint and replacing them with artificial components designed to restore joint function. The procedure is considered based on several factors rather than age alone. Symptoms, functional limitations, joint damage, overall health, previous treatments, and individual expectations are all relevant.</p>\n<p>Before recommending surgery, an orthopedic evaluation is important. The doctor may assess walking pattern, joint movement, stability, muscle strength, pain levels, and other symptoms. X-rays can help show changes in the joint and assist with treatment planning.</p>\n<p>Modern orthopedic surgery also includes technology-assisted approaches for selected patients. Robotic-assisted joint replacement uses digital planning and technology to support the surgeon during specific stages of the procedure. It is important to understand that the surgeon remains responsible for the operation and determines the appropriate surgical plan.</p>\n<p>At Srikara Hospitals, Lakdikapul, Dr. Jagadeesh Mavilla is a Consultant Robotic Joint Replacement Surgeon with expertise in arthroscopy, sports medicine, and complex trauma. Patients experiencing advanced knee problems can discuss their symptoms and available treatment options during an orthopedic consultation.</p>\n<p>Recovery after knee replacement involves more than the surgical procedure itself. Rehabilitation is an important part of the treatment journey. Physiotherapy may focus on restoring knee movement, improving muscle strength, developing balance, and gradually returning to everyday activities.</p>\n<p>Patients should also understand that recovery varies. Factors such as age, muscle strength, general health, activity level, and adherence to rehabilitation can influence progress. Following postoperative instructions and attending scheduled reviews are important components of recovery.</p>\n<p>Not every person with knee arthritis needs a replacement. Surgery is generally considered when symptoms and functional limitations are significant and other appropriate treatment approaches have not provided sufficient relief. A personalized medical assessment is therefore essential before making a decision.</p>\n<p>Lifestyle measures can continue to support joint health even after treatment. Maintaining an appropriate body weight, staying physically active within recommended limits, strengthening the muscles around the knee, and avoiding unnecessary strain can help support mobility.</p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Severe knee arthritis can have a substantial effect on everyday life, but treatment options are available. Early management may help control symptoms, while knee replacement can be considered for selected patients with advanced joint damage and significant functional limitations. A detailed orthopedic evaluation can help determine the most appropriate treatment approach for each individual.</p>\n
    `
