    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/rameshwari-vishwakarma-blog-2.jpg',
    content: `
<p>High blood pressure, also known as hypertension, is one of the most common cardiovascular risk factors. It occurs when the force of blood against the walls of the arteries remains elevated over time. Because hypertension often produces no obvious symptoms, many people may have it without realizing it.</p>\n<p>Blood pressure naturally changes throughout the day depending on activity, stress, sleep, and other factors. However, consistently elevated blood pressure can place additional strain on the heart and blood vessels.</p>\n<p>The heart pumps blood through arteries to deliver oxygen and nutrients throughout the body. When blood pressure remains high for long periods, the heart has to work harder to circulate blood. Over time, this increased workload can contribute to changes in the heart and blood vessels.</p>\n<p>Uncontrolled hypertension can increase the risk of cardiovascular complications. It is associated with conditions such as coronary artery disease, heart failure, stroke, and other problems affecting blood vessels. It can also contribute to kidney damage and other organ complications.</p>\n<p>One of the challenges with hypertension is the absence of symptoms in many patients. A person may feel completely healthy even when their blood pressure is elevated. Headaches, dizziness, or other symptoms can occur in some situations, but symptoms alone cannot reliably indicate whether blood pressure is high.</p>\n<p>Regular blood pressure measurement is therefore important. People may have their blood pressure checked during routine medical appointments or use validated home monitoring devices when recommended.</p>\n<p>Several lifestyle factors can influence blood pressure. Excessive salt intake, being physically inactive, excess body weight, smoking, excessive alcohol consumption, poor sleep, and prolonged stress can contribute to cardiovascular risk. Family history and age can also influence the likelihood of developing hypertension.</p>\n<p>Dietary changes can be helpful. A balanced diet that includes vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, and other nutrient-rich foods can support cardiovascular health. Reducing excessive salt and highly processed foods may also be beneficial.</p>\n<p>Regular physical activity is another important component. The appropriate type and amount of exercise depends on an individual's health status and should be discussed with a healthcare professional when necessary.</p>\n<p>Some patients require medication to control blood pressure. Different classes of medicines work in different ways, and treatment is selected according to factors such as blood pressure levels, age, kidney function, diabetes, cardiovascular risk, and other health considerations.</p>\n<p>Dr. Rameshwari Vishwakarma, MBBS, MD, DM Cardiology, is a Consultant Interventional Cardiologist who provides cardiovascular evaluation and management as part of specialized cardiac care.</p>\n<p>Patients who have been prescribed blood pressure medication should not stop or change the dose without discussing it with their healthcare professional. Blood pressure may improve with treatment even when the patient feels no different, making continued monitoring important.</p>\n<p>Extremely high blood pressure accompanied by symptoms such as severe chest pain, significant breathlessness, neurological changes, severe headache, confusion, or vision problems requires urgent medical assessment.</p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>High blood pressure can remain unnoticed while gradually increasing cardiovascular risk. Regular monitoring, healthy lifestyle habits, appropriate medical evaluation, and prescribed treatment can help manage hypertension. Understanding blood pressure and taking it seriously can be an important step toward protecting long-term heart and blood vessel health.</p>\n
    `
