    doctorSlug: 'rameshwari-vishwakarma',
    slug: 'understanding-coronary-angioplasty-and-stent-place',
    title: `Understanding Coronary Angioplasty and Stent Placement`,
    excerpt: `Coronary artery disease occurs when the blood vessels supplying the heart become narrowed or blocked. This narrowing can reduce blood flow to the heart muscle and may cause symptoms such as chest discomfort or breathlessness. In certain patients, coronary angioplasty and stent placement may be recommended to restore blood flow.`,
    category: 'Cardiology',
    date: '111 Sep 2026',
    readTime: '4 min read',
    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/rameshwari-vishwakarma-blog-4.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Coronary artery disease occurs when the blood vessels supplying the heart become narrowed or blocked. This narrowing can reduce blood flow to the heart muscle and may cause symptoms such as chest discomfort or breathlessness. In certain patients, coronary angioplasty and stent placement may be recommended to restore blood flow.</p>\n<p>The coronary arteries are blood vessels that supply oxygen-rich blood to the heart muscle. Over time, fatty deposits and other substances can build up within the artery walls. This process can cause the arteries to become narrower and restrict blood flow.</p>\n<p>When a coronary artery becomes significantly narrowed, a patient may develop angina. Symptoms can occur during physical exertion when the heart needs more oxygen. In some cases, a sudden blockage caused by a blood clot can result in a heart attack.</p>\n<p>Coronary angioplasty, also known as percutaneous coronary intervention or PCI, is a catheter-based procedure used to open certain narrowed or blocked coronary arteries. It is performed by an interventional cardiology team in an appropriately equipped cardiac facility.</p>\n<p>During the procedure, a thin catheter is guided through a blood vessel toward the heart. A small balloon may be positioned at the site of the narrowing and inflated to widen the artery. In many cases, a stent is placed to help keep the artery open.</p>\n<p>A stent is a small mesh-like structure that remains inside the artery. Modern stents are commonly designed to release medication that helps reduce the risk of the artery becoming narrowed again.</p>\n<p>Not every person with coronary artery disease requires angioplasty or a stent. The decision depends on factors such as the location and severity of the blockage, symptoms, test results, the patient's overall health, and whether medical treatment is sufficient.</p>\n<p>Doctors may use coronary angiography to identify blockages and understand the anatomy of the coronary arteries. Other investigations may also be used to assess heart function and determine the most suitable treatment approach.</p>\n<p>Angioplasty can be performed in emergency situations, including certain types of heart attacks, where rapidly restoring blood flow may be an important part of treatment. It may also be planned for selected patients with significant coronary artery disease.</p>\n<p>Dr. Rameshwari Vishwakarma, MBBS, MD, DM Cardiology, is a Consultant Interventional Cardiologist specializing in cardiovascular evaluation and interventional cardiac care.</p>\n<p>After angioplasty, patients are generally prescribed medicines to reduce the risk of blood clots and manage cardiovascular risk factors. Taking prescribed medications exactly as advised is important. Patients should not stop antiplatelet medication without medical guidance because doing so can increase the risk of complications.</p>\n<p>Lifestyle management remains important even after successful angioplasty. The procedure treats a specific blockage but does not eliminate the underlying factors that contribute to coronary artery disease. Blood pressure, cholesterol, diabetes, weight, smoking, diet, and physical activity may all need attention.</p>\n<p>Cardiac rehabilitation and gradual return to physical activity may be recommended depending on the patient's condition and treatment.</p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Coronary angioplasty and stent placement are important interventional treatments for selected patients with narrowed or blocked coronary arteries. The procedure can help restore blood flow in appropriate situations, including certain heart attacks. Long-term heart health also depends on medication adherence, risk-factor management, lifestyle changes, and regular medical follow-up.</p>\n
    `
