    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/rameshwari-vishwakarma-blog-5.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Heart health deserves attention at every stage of life, but cardiovascular risk can change as people grow older. After the age of 40, maintaining healthy lifestyle habits and monitoring important health indicators can become increasingly important for reducing cardiovascular risk.</p>\n<p>Heart disease does not always begin with obvious symptoms. Conditions such as high blood pressure, diabetes, and high cholesterol may develop silently. Over time, these factors can affect the arteries and increase the risk of coronary artery disease.</p>\n<p>Regular health checks can help identify these risks. Blood pressure measurement, blood sugar testing, cholesterol evaluation, weight assessment, and other investigations may be recommended based on individual health and risk factors.</p>\n<p>Diet plays an important role in cardiovascular health. A balanced eating pattern can include vegetables, fruits, whole grains, pulses, nuts, seeds, and appropriate sources of protein. Reducing excessive intake of salt, added sugars, highly processed foods, and unhealthy fats can support overall heart health.</p>\n<p>Physical activity is another important factor. Regular movement can support cardiovascular fitness, muscle strength, weight management, and metabolic health. People who have existing heart conditions or other medical concerns should discuss appropriate exercise levels with their healthcare professional.</p>\n<p>Smoking is a major cardiovascular risk factor. Tobacco exposure can damage blood vessels and contribute to the development of coronary artery disease. Quitting smoking can be an important step toward reducing cardiovascular risk.</p>\n<p>Stress and sleep can also influence overall health. Chronic stress may affect eating habits, physical activity, blood pressure, and other health behaviors. Adequate, consistent sleep supports general physical and cardiovascular well-being.</p>\n<p>Diabetes requires particular attention because persistently elevated blood sugar can damage blood vessels and contribute to cardiovascular disease. People with diabetes should follow their treatment plan and monitor blood sugar as advised.</p>\n<p>High cholesterol is another important factor. Cholesterol is necessary for several body functions, but elevated levels of certain types of cholesterol can contribute to plaque formation within arteries. Treatment may include dietary changes, physical activity, and medication when prescribed.</p>\n<p>Family history can also provide useful information about cardiovascular risk. Individuals with close relatives who developed heart disease at a younger age may benefit from discussing their personal risk factors with a healthcare professional.</p>\n<p>Symptoms should never be ignored simply because a person is relatively young or has never experienced heart problems before. Chest pressure, unexplained breathlessness, sudden weakness, fainting, unusual sweating, or discomfort spreading to the arm, jaw, back, or shoulder can require urgent medical evaluation.</p>\n<p>Dr. Rameshwari Vishwakarma, MBBS, MD, DM Cardiology, is a Consultant Interventional Cardiologist involved in specialized cardiac evaluation and management.</p>\n<p>People who already have cardiovascular disease may require long-term monitoring and treatment. Prescribed medicines should be taken as directed, and follow-up appointments should be maintained so that treatment can be reviewed when necessary.</p>\n<p>Preventive cardiology is not about making drastic changes overnight. Small, consistent improvements in food choices, physical activity, tobacco avoidance, sleep, weight management, and regular health monitoring can contribute to healthier habits over time.</p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Protecting heart health after 40 involves more than responding to symptoms. Regular health checks, balanced nutrition, physical activity, tobacco avoidance, adequate sleep, stress management, and proper control of blood pressure, cholesterol, and blood sugar can all support cardiovascular health. Early awareness and appropriate medical guidance can help people take informed steps toward protecting their heart.</p>\n
    `
