    slug: 'diabetes-and-kidney-disease-how-blood-sugar-can-af',
    title: `Diabetes and Kidney Disease: How Blood Sugar Can Affect Kidney Function`,
    excerpt: `Diabetes can affect several organs when blood glucose remains elevated over a long period. The kidneys are among the organs that may be affected because they contain a network of very small blood vessels responsible for filtering the blood.`,
    category: 'Nephrology',
    date: '111 Sep 2026',
    readTime: '4 min read',
    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/vaishnavi-p-blog-4.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Diabetes can affect several organs when blood glucose remains elevated over a long period. The kidneys are among the organs that may be affected because they contain a network of very small blood vessels responsible for filtering the blood.</p>\n<p>Kidney damage associated with diabetes can develop gradually. In its early stages, a person may not experience noticeable symptoms. Regular monitoring of blood sugar, blood pressure and kidney function can therefore be an important part of diabetes care.</p>\n<h3>Why Diabetes Can Affect the Kidneys</h3>\n<p>The kidneys filter large amounts of blood every day. Their filtering units contain tiny blood vessels that are sensitive to long-term changes caused by diabetes.</p>\n<p>Persistently high blood glucose can damage these structures over time. As the filtering system becomes affected, proteins such as albumin may begin to appear in the urine.</p>\n<p>Continued kidney damage can eventually reduce the kidneys' ability to remove waste and maintain fluid and mineral balance.</p>\n<h3>Early Kidney Damage May Be Difficult to Notice</h3>\n<p>One of the challenges with diabetes-related kidney disease is that it may not produce obvious symptoms initially.</p>\n<p>A person can have early kidney changes while continuing to feel well. Waiting for symptoms before getting tested may therefore delay identification of kidney problems.</p>\n<p>Urine and blood tests can help detect changes that may not yet be apparent from physical symptoms.</p>\n<h3>Tests That Help Monitor Kidney Health</h3>\n<p>People with diabetes may be advised to undergo regular kidney assessments based on their individual medical circumstances.</p>\n<p>Urine testing can measure albumin, a protein that may appear in the urine when the kidney's filtering barrier becomes affected.</p>\n<p>Blood testing can measure creatinine and help calculate estimated glomerular filtration rate, or eGFR. This provides information about kidney filtering function.</p>\n<p>Blood pressure monitoring is also important because hypertension can increase the risk of kidney damage in people with diabetes.</p>\n<h3>Factors That Can Increase Kidney Risk</h3>\n<p>Blood sugar control is only one part of protecting kidney health. High blood pressure, smoking, excess body weight, cardiovascular disease and other medical conditions can also influence kidney risk.</p>\n<p>People with diabetes should discuss their individual risk factors with their healthcare provider rather than assuming that normal day-to-day symptoms indicate normal kidney function.</p>\n<h3>Supporting Kidney Health With Diabetes</h3>\n<p>Keeping blood glucose within the range recommended by a healthcare professional is an important part of diabetes management.</p>\n<p>Blood pressure should also be monitored and treated when necessary. A balanced diet, regular physical activity, appropriate weight management and avoidance of tobacco can contribute to overall health.</p>\n<p>Medication should be taken exactly as prescribed. Patients should also tell their healthcare team about all medicines, supplements and over-the-counter products they use, because some products may require special consideration in people with reduced kidney function.</p>\n<p>Dietary recommendations may change depending on kidney function and other medical conditions. Therefore, major dietary restrictions should not be started without appropriate medical guidance.</p>\n<h3>Why Screening Should Not Be Delayed</h3>\n<p>Kidney testing is useful even when a person with diabetes feels healthy. Early changes can provide an opportunity for healthcare professionals to review blood sugar control, blood pressure, medications and other risk factors.</p>\n<p>The frequency of testing depends on the individual's diabetes history, kidney function and overall health.</p>\n<p>If kidney function becomes significantly reduced, specialised nephrology care may be required. In advanced disease, treatment options such as dialysis or kidney transplantation may be considered depending on the patient's circumstances.</p>\n<p>Dr. Vaishnavi Pochineni, MBBS, MD (USA), FACP (USA), FASN (USA), Transplant Fellowship – Mayo Clinic, and American Board Certified in Internal Medicine & Nephrology, is a Consultant Nephrologist & Kidney Transplant Physician who provides specialised evaluation and management for kidney conditions.</p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Diabetes-related kidney damage can develop silently, which makes regular monitoring particularly important. Managing blood glucose and blood pressure, attending recommended kidney screenings and following personalised medical advice can help identify kidney changes early and support long-term health.</p>\n
    `
