    slug: 'acute-kidney-injury-causes-warning-signs-and-the-i',
    title: `Acute Kidney Injury: Causes, Warning Signs and the Importance of Prompt Treatment`,
    excerpt: `The kidneys normally remove waste products from the blood and help maintain the body's fluid, electrolyte and chemical balance. Sometimes, however, kidney function can decrease rapidly over a short period. This condition is known as acute kidney injury, or AKI.`,
    category: 'Nephrology',
    date: '112 Sep 2026',
    readTime: '4 min read',
    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/vaishnavi-p-blog-5.jpg',
    content: `
<p>The kidneys normally remove waste products from the blood and help maintain the body's fluid, electrolyte and chemical balance. Sometimes, however, kidney function can decrease rapidly over a short period. This condition is known as acute kidney injury, or AKI.</p>\n<p>Unlike chronic kidney disease, which develops over a longer period, acute kidney injury can occur suddenly. It may happen during a serious illness, after significant fluid loss or because of certain medicines and medical conditions. Early recognition and treatment are important because the underlying cause may sometimes be reversible.</p>\n<h3>What Is Acute Kidney Injury?</h3>\n<p>Acute kidney injury refers to a sudden decline in kidney function. When the kidneys are unable to filter the blood normally, waste products and excess fluid can accumulate.</p>\n<p>AKI can range from a mild change in kidney function to a severe condition requiring close medical supervision.</p>\n<p>It may occur in people who previously had normal kidneys, but individuals with existing kidney disease or other serious health conditions can also be at increased risk.</p>\n<h3>Common Causes of AKI</h3>\n<p>Several situations can contribute to acute kidney injury.</p>\n<p>Severe dehydration caused by vomiting, diarrhoea, fever or inadequate fluid intake can reduce blood flow to the kidneys.</p>\n<p>Serious infections and conditions that cause a significant drop in blood pressure may also affect kidney function.</p>\n<p>Certain medicines can contribute to kidney injury in susceptible individuals, particularly when used under specific circumstances or alongside dehydration and other illnesses.</p>\n<p>Blockage of urine flow is another possible cause. Problems affecting the urinary tract may prevent urine from leaving the body normally and can place pressure on the kidneys.</p>\n<h3>Possible Warning Signs</h3>\n<p>The symptoms of AKI can vary depending on its severity and underlying cause.</p>\n<p>Some people may notice that they are producing much less urine than usual. Others may develop swelling in the legs, ankles or around the eyes because of fluid retention.</p>\n<p>Tiredness, nausea, confusion, breathlessness or changes in general health can also occur in more serious cases.</p>\n<p>However, AKI may sometimes be detected through blood tests before significant symptoms become obvious.</p>\n<h3>How Is Acute Kidney Injury Diagnosed?</h3>\n<p>Doctors may review the patient's medical history, recent illnesses, fluid intake, medicines and other relevant factors.</p>\n<p>Blood tests can measure creatinine and other substances to assess changes in kidney function. Urine tests can provide additional information about how the kidneys are functioning and may help identify possible causes.</p>\n<p>Depending on the clinical situation, imaging tests such as an ultrasound may be used to look for urinary obstruction or other abnormalities.</p>\n<h3>Treatment Depends on the Cause</h3>\n<p>There is no single treatment that applies to every case of AKI. Management focuses on identifying and addressing the underlying problem.</p>\n<p>If dehydration is responsible, appropriate fluid management may be required. If an infection or another medical condition is contributing to kidney injury, treating that condition becomes an important part of care.</p>\n<p>Doctors may also review medicines that could affect kidney function and make changes when medically appropriate.</p>\n<p>If urine flow is blocked, treatment may be necessary to relieve the obstruction.</p>\n<p>In severe cases, temporary dialysis may be required while the kidneys recover or while the underlying condition is being treated.</p>\n<h3>Can AKI Be Prevented?</h3>\n<p>Not every episode of acute kidney injury can be prevented. However, some risks can be reduced by maintaining appropriate hydration during illnesses when medically suitable, avoiding unnecessary self-medication and informing healthcare professionals about existing kidney problems.</p>\n<p>People with chronic kidney disease, diabetes, heart conditions or other medical problems may need additional precautions during serious illnesses or medical procedures.</p>\n<p>Dr. Vaishnavi Pochineni, MBBS, MD (USA), FACP (USA), FASN (USA), Transplant Fellowship – Mayo Clinic, and American Board Certified in Internal Medicine & Nephrology, is a Consultant Nephrologist & Kidney Transplant Physician who evaluates and manages a range of acute and chronic kidney conditions.</p>\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>Acute kidney injury can develop quickly and may require timely medical attention. Reduced urine output, swelling or significant changes in health during an illness should not be ignored. Early evaluation can help identify the cause, guide treatment and provide the kidneys with the best possible opportunity for recovery.</p>\n
    `
