    readTime: '4 min read',
    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/ch-manohar-blog-1.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Your heartbeat usually goes unnoticed. But there are moments when you may suddenly become aware of your heart beating faster, harder, or differently than usual. You might feel a flutter in your chest, a sudden pounding sensation, or what seems like a skipped beat. These experiences are commonly described as heart palpitations.</p>
<p>Most palpitations are not dangerous and may happen after exercise, emotional stress, poor sleep, or consuming too much caffeine. However, repeated or unexplained episodes can sometimes be linked to an abnormal heart rhythm or another health condition and should be evaluated appropriately.</p>
<p>Dr. CH. Manohar, MBBS, PGDCC (Cardiology), CCBDM, Consultant Clinical Cardiologist, provides cardiac assessment for patients experiencing symptoms related to heart rhythm and overall cardiovascular health.</p>
<h3>What Exactly Are Heart Palpitations?</h3>
<p>Palpitations are not a disease by themselves. They describe the sensation of being unusually aware of your heartbeat.</p>
<h3>A person may experience:</h3>
<h3>* A racing heartbeat</h3>
<h3>* Strong or forceful heartbeats</h3>
<h3>* Fluttering in the chest</h3>
<h3>* A feeling that the heart has paused briefly</h3>
<h3>* Irregular or uneven beats</h3>
<h3>* A pounding sensation in the neck or chest</h3>
<p>The episode may last only a few seconds or continue for several minutes. Its timing and associated symptoms can provide useful clues about the possible cause.</p>
<h3>Why Do Palpitations Happen?</h3>
<p>There are numerous reasons why the heart may temporarily beat differently.</p>
<h3>Common triggers include:</h3>
<h3>* Emotional stress</h3>
<h3>* Anxiety</h3>
<h3>* Excess caffeine</h3>
<h3>* Lack of adequate sleep</h3>
<h3>* Dehydration</h3>
<h3>* Fever</h3>
<h3>* Intense exercise</h3>
<h3>* Smoking</h3>
<h3>* Certain medicines</h3>
<h3>* Hormonal changes</h3>
<p>Palpitations may also occur because of medical conditions such as thyroid disorders, anaemia, electrolyte abnormalities, or disturbances in the heart's electrical system.</p>
<h3>Are All Palpitations Related to Heart Disease?</h3>
<p>No. Feeling your heartbeat does not automatically mean that you have heart disease.</p>
<p>For example, someone may notice a rapid heartbeat after climbing several flights of stairs. Similarly, anxiety can activate the body's stress response and temporarily increase the heart rate.</p>
<p>The concern increases when palpitations happen repeatedly without an obvious trigger, become progressively more frequent, or occur alongside other symptoms.</p>
<h3>Warning Signs You Should Not Ignore</h3>
<p>Palpitations deserve prompt medical attention when they are accompanied by symptoms suggesting reduced blood flow or an unstable heart rhythm.</p>
<h3>Important warning signs include:</h3>
<h3>* Chest discomfort</h3>
<h3>* Difficulty breathing</h3>
<h3>* Severe dizziness</h3>
<h3>* Fainting or near-fainting</h3>
<h3>* Unusual sweating</h3>
<h3>* Significant weakness</h3>
<h3>* A very rapid heartbeat that does not settle</h3>
<p>If these symptoms appear suddenly or are severe, emergency medical care may be necessary.</p>
<h3>How Is the Problem Investigated?</h3>
<p>A doctor will usually begin by understanding what the heartbeat feels like, how long the episodes last, what you were doing when they occurred, and whether any other symptoms appeared.</p>
<p>Depending on the situation, investigations may include an ECG, blood tests, echocardiography, or prolonged rhythm monitoring.</p>
<p>A Holter monitor can be helpful when symptoms come and go because it records heart activity over a longer period than a standard ECG.</p>
<h3>What Can You Do to Reduce Episodes?</h3>
<p>If a serious underlying cause is not found, identifying personal triggers can be helpful.</p>
<h3>You may benefit from:</h3>
<h3>* Maintaining a regular sleep schedule</h3>
<h3>* Drinking enough fluids</h3>
<h3>* Reducing excessive caffeine</h3>
<h3>* Avoiding tobacco</h3>
<h3>* Managing stress</h3>
<h3>* Staying physically active as appropriate</h3>
<h3>* Following a balanced eating pattern</h3>
<h3>* Taking prescribed medicines correctly</h3>
<p>Keeping a simple note of when palpitations happen can also help identify patterns.</p>
<h3>Conclusion</h3>
<p>Heart palpitations can be harmless in many situations, but recurring or unexplained changes in heartbeat deserve attention. Symptoms such as chest discomfort, breathlessness, fainting, or severe dizziness should not be overlooked.</p>
<p>Understanding the pattern of your symptoms and getting the right cardiac evaluation can help distinguish occasional harmless palpitations from problems that require treatment.</p>
`
