    title: `Head Injury and Brain Trauma: Warning Signs That Require Immediate Medical Attention`,
    excerpt: `Head injuries can happen during road accidents, sports activities, falls, workplace incidents, or everyday situations. Some head injuries are minor and resolve with observation and appropriate care, while others can result in serious damage to the brain. Recognizing warning signs after a head injury is therefore extremely important.`,
    category: 'Plastic Surgery',
    date: '18 Sep 2026',
    readTime: '4 min read',
    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/kota-ravi-chandra-blog-4.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Head injuries can happen during road accidents, sports activities, falls, workplace incidents, or everyday situations. Some head injuries are minor and resolve with observation and appropriate care, while others can result in serious damage to the brain. Recognizing warning signs after a head injury is therefore extremely important.</p>
<p>A head injury can affect the scalp, skull, or brain. A concussion is a form of traumatic brain injury that can temporarily affect brain function. More serious injuries may involve bleeding inside the skull, swelling of the brain, skull fractures, or damage to brain tissue.</p>
<p>Symptoms after a head injury can vary. A person may experience headache, dizziness, nausea, vomiting, confusion, tiredness, sensitivity to light, memory problems, or difficulty concentrating. Some symptoms may appear immediately, while others can develop or worsen over time.</p>
<p>Certain warning signs require urgent medical assessment. Repeated vomiting, increasing or severe headache, loss of consciousness, seizures, unusual sleepiness, confusion, difficulty speaking, weakness or numbness, problems with balance, unequal pupils, or changes in vision can indicate a more serious injury.</p>
<p>Bleeding inside the skull can sometimes occur even when there is no obvious external wound. Older adults and people taking certain blood-thinning medications may require particular attention after a head injury because the risk of significant bleeding can be higher in some circumstances.</p>
<p>Children can also sustain head injuries, and their symptoms may not always be easy to identify. Changes in behavior, unusual crying, excessive sleepiness, repeated vomiting, difficulty walking, or reduced responsiveness should be evaluated promptly.</p>
<p>The initial assessment usually includes a detailed history and neurological examination. The healthcare professional may ask how the injury occurred, whether there was loss of consciousness, whether symptoms developed afterward, and whether the person takes any medications that could influence bleeding risk.</p>
<p>Imaging such as a CT scan may be recommended when doctors suspect bleeding, skull fracture, or another significant injury. MRI may be useful in selected situations, depending on the clinical circumstances.</p>
<p>Treatment depends on the severity and type of injury. Minor injuries may require observation, rest, symptom management, and follow-up. More serious injuries may require hospitalization, close neurological monitoring, medication, or surgical intervention.</p>
<p>Surgery can sometimes be necessary when there is significant bleeding or pressure on the brain. The specific approach depends on the type, location, and size of the abnormality and the patient's overall condition.</p>
<p>People recovering from a concussion or other brain injury should follow medical advice regarding physical and mental activity. Returning to sports, driving, work, or strenuous activities too quickly may not be appropriate for everyone.</p>
<p>Prevention remains an important part of reducing traumatic brain injuries. Wearing helmets during appropriate sports and two-wheeler travel, using seat belts, following road safety practices, maintaining safe environments at home, and taking precautions at workplaces can reduce injury risks.</p>
<p>It is also important not to assume that a person is fine simply because they appear conscious immediately after an accident. Some complications may develop later, making observation and medical advice important when symptoms or risk factors are present.</p>
<p>Dr. Kota Ravi Chandra, MBBS, MS, MCh (Neurosurgery), Consultant Brain & Spine Surgeon, can assess brain and spine-related injuries and guide patients regarding appropriate neurological evaluation and treatment.</p>
<p>After any significant head injury, especially one involving loss of consciousness or concerning symptoms, timely medical assessment is essential. Recognizing warning signs early can help identify serious complications and ensure that appropriate treatment is provided without unnecessary delay.</p>
`
