    title: `Piles and Hemorrhoids: Symptoms, Causes, and Modern Treatment Options`,
    excerpt: `Piles, medically known as hemorrhoids, are swollen blood vessels and supporting tissues in the lower rectum and anus. They are a common condition that can affect adults of different ages. Although piles are usually not life-threatening, symptoms such as bleeding, pain, itching, or swelling can interfere with everyday comfort and quality of life.`,
    category: 'General Surgery',
    date: '18 Sep 2026',
    readTime: '4 min read',
    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/hemanth-kumar-blog-3.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Piles, medically known as hemorrhoids, are swollen blood vessels and supporting tissues in the lower rectum and anus. They are a common condition that can affect adults of different ages. Although piles are usually not life-threatening, symptoms such as bleeding, pain, itching, or swelling can interfere with everyday comfort and quality of life.</p>
<p>Hemorrhoids can be broadly classified as internal or external. Internal hemorrhoids develop inside the rectum, while external hemorrhoids occur beneath the skin around the anus. Internal piles may cause painless bleeding, particularly during bowel movements, while external piles can sometimes produce pain, swelling, or tenderness.</p>
<p>Several factors can increase the likelihood of developing hemorrhoids. Constipation and repeated straining during bowel movements are common contributors. Spending prolonged periods sitting on the toilet, a low-fiber diet, pregnancy, obesity, and persistent diarrhea may also contribute. Heavy lifting and increased pressure within the abdomen can sometimes worsen symptoms.</p>
<p>One of the common symptoms is bright-red blood noticed during or after passing stool. Other symptoms may include itching, irritation, discomfort, swelling, or a feeling that the bowel has not completely emptied.</p>
<p>However, rectal bleeding should never automatically be assumed to be piles. Other conditions, including gastrointestinal disorders and certain cancers, can also cause bleeding. Therefore, persistent or unexplained bleeding should be evaluated by a qualified medical professional.</p>
<p>For mild hemorrhoid symptoms, lifestyle changes may provide relief. Increasing dietary fiber through vegetables, fruits, whole grains, and other fiber-rich foods can help produce softer stools. Adequate water intake and regular physical activity may also support healthy bowel movements.</p>
<p>Avoiding unnecessary straining and reducing prolonged sitting on the toilet can also help. Some patients may require medications or topical treatments depending on their symptoms and medical evaluation.</p>
<p>When symptoms are persistent, recurrent, or severe, procedural or surgical treatment may be considered. The choice depends on the type and severity of hemorrhoids and the patient's overall condition.</p>
<p>Modern surgical and minimally invasive approaches aim to treat symptomatic hemorrhoids while considering patient comfort and recovery. Laser-based procedures may be considered in selected cases, but the suitability of any technique should be determined after examination.</p>
<p>A common misconception is that every case of piles requires surgery. Many patients can manage mild symptoms through conservative treatment. At the same time, repeatedly postponing treatment for significant symptoms may allow the condition to become more troublesome.</p>
<p>Patients should seek medical attention when bleeding is recurrent, pain is severe, a lump becomes increasingly swollen, symptoms interfere with normal activities, or home measures fail to provide improvement.</p>
<p>Diagnosis generally involves a medical history and examination. Depending on age, symptoms, bleeding pattern, and other risk factors, additional tests may sometimes be recommended to rule out other causes.</p>
<p>Dr. Hemanth Kumar, MBBS, DNB (General Surgery), FMAS, FIAGES, FISCP, FALS – Hernia, Consultant General, Laser & Laparoscopic Surgeon, can evaluate symptoms related to piles and advise appropriate medical, minimally invasive, or surgical treatment when required.</p>
<p>Preventing constipation is one of the most useful ways to reduce the risk of hemorrhoid symptoms. A fiber-rich diet, sufficient hydration, regular movement, and healthy bowel habits can support digestive health.</p>
<p>Piles are common, but persistent rectal bleeding or severe symptoms should not be self-diagnosed. Proper evaluation can identify the actual cause and help determine whether lifestyle changes, medication, a procedure, or surgery is appropriate.</p>
`
