    title: `Gallstones: Symptoms, Complications, and When Gallbladder Surgery May Be Needed`,
    excerpt: `Gallstones are hardened deposits that develop inside the gallbladder. They can range in size from very small particles to larger stones and may occur in people of different age groups. Some people have gallstones without experiencing any symptoms, while others may develop significant abdominal pain and digestive problems.`,
    category: 'General Surgery',
    date: '18 Sep 2026',
    readTime: '4 min read',
    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/hemanth-kumar-blog-4.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Gallstones are hardened deposits that develop inside the gallbladder. They can range in size from very small particles to larger stones and may occur in people of different age groups. Some people have gallstones without experiencing any symptoms, while others may develop significant abdominal pain and digestive problems.</p>
<p>The gallbladder is a small organ located beneath the liver. It stores bile, a digestive fluid produced by the liver. Bile helps the body digest fats. Gallstones can form when substances in bile, such as cholesterol or bilirubin, become imbalanced and crystallize.</p>
<p>Not everyone with gallstones requires treatment. Stones that do not cause symptoms may sometimes be discovered incidentally during an ultrasound performed for another reason. However, symptomatic gallstones can cause repeated episodes of pain and may require medical evaluation.</p>
<p>Gallstone pain is often felt in the upper right or upper middle part of the abdomen. It may occur after eating, particularly after a heavy or fatty meal, and can sometimes radiate toward the back or right shoulder. Nausea and vomiting may accompany an episode.</p>
<p>Gallstones can sometimes block the flow of bile. This may result in inflammation of the gallbladder, known as cholecystitis. A blocked bile duct can cause jaundice, dark urine, pale stools, or significant abdominal discomfort. Infection of the bile ducts can also occur and requires urgent medical attention.</p>
<p>Symptoms such as severe or persistent abdominal pain, fever, repeated vomiting, yellowing of the eyes or skin, or chills should not be ignored. Prompt medical assessment is important because complications can sometimes become serious.</p>
<p>Ultrasound is commonly used to identify gallstones and assess the gallbladder. Depending on symptoms and suspected complications, doctors may recommend blood tests or additional imaging.</p>
<p>When gallstones cause repeated symptoms or complications, removal of the gallbladder may be recommended. The most common surgical approach is laparoscopic cholecystectomy. During this procedure, the gallbladder is removed through small abdominal incisions using specialized instruments and a camera.</p>
<p>The body can generally continue to digest food without the gallbladder because the liver continues to produce bile. After surgery, some patients may temporarily notice changes in digestion, although many return to their normal diet and routine as recovery progresses.</p>
<p>As with any surgical procedure, gallbladder surgery has potential risks, including bleeding, infection, injury to nearby structures, and reactions to anesthesia. The surgeon evaluates the patient's condition and medical history before recommending surgery.</p>
<p>Dietary changes may help some people manage symptoms temporarily, particularly avoiding foods that consistently trigger pain. However, dietary management does not remove existing gallstones. Patients with recurrent symptoms should discuss treatment options with a doctor rather than relying exclusively on diet.</p>
<p>Dr. Hemanth Kumar, MBBS, DNB (General Surgery), FMAS, FIAGES, FISCP, FALS – Hernia, Consultant General, Laser & Laparoscopic Surgeon, can evaluate gallstone-related symptoms and advise patients regarding appropriate investigations and surgical options.</p>
<p>Early diagnosis can help identify gallstones before complications develop. People experiencing recurrent upper abdominal pain, particularly after meals, should seek medical evaluation rather than repeatedly treating the symptoms at home.</p>
<p>Gallstones are common and manageable when appropriately diagnosed. Understanding the symptoms, recognizing emergency warning signs, and receiving timely medical advice can help patients make informed decisions about treatment and recovery.</p>
`
