    title: `Diabetes Warning Signs: Symptoms That May Appear Before a Diagnosis`,
    excerpt: `Diabetes can develop without creating a noticeable change in how a person feels. This is one reason it is often identified during a routine blood test rather than because of a specific complaint. However, when blood glucose remains elevated, the body may begin showing subtle changes. Recognizing these changes can encourage people to get tested before the condition progresses.`,
    category: 'General Medicine',
    date: '18 Sep 2026',
    readTime: '4 min read',
    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/karunakar-blog-1.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Diabetes can develop without creating a noticeable change in how a person feels. This is one reason it is often identified during a routine blood test rather than because of a specific complaint. However, when blood glucose remains elevated, the body may begin showing subtle changes. Recognizing these changes can encourage people to get tested before the condition progresses.</p>
<p>Dr. Karunakar, MBBS, MD (General Medicine), Consultant General Physician & Diabetologist, provides medical consultation for diabetes, blood sugar disorders, and general health concerns, with an emphasis on timely assessment and appropriate long-term management.</p>
<h3>Why Does Diabetes Develop?</h3>
<p>The body normally converts food into glucose, which provides energy to cells. Insulin helps move glucose from the bloodstream into the cells.</p>
<p>Diabetes can occur when the body does not make sufficient insulin, does not respond to insulin effectively, or both. As a result, glucose can remain in the bloodstream at higher-than-normal levels.</p>
<p>If this continues for an extended period, elevated blood sugar can affect blood vessels, nerves, kidneys, eyes, and other parts of the body.</p>
<h3>Changes You May Notice</h3>
<p>Some people with diabetes experience symptoms that develop gradually rather than suddenly.</p>
<h3>Possible changes include:</h3>
<h3>* Drinking water much more often than usual</h3>
<h3>* Passing urine frequently, especially at night</h3>
<h3>* Feeling hungry soon after meals</h3>
<h3>* Losing weight without trying</h3>
<h3>* Feeling drained or lacking energy</h3>
<h3>* Experiencing recurring infections</h3>
<h3>* Noticing cuts take longer to heal</h3>
<h3>* Developing blurred vision</h3>
<h3>* Experiencing dry or itchy skin</h3>
<p>Having one of these symptoms does not confirm diabetes. The same complaints can occur with other medical conditions.</p>
<h3>Why Does Frequent Urination Occur?</h3>
<p>When glucose levels become excessively high, the kidneys attempt to remove some of the extra glucose through urine. This can lead to increased urination.</p>
<p>As the body loses more fluid, thirst may increase as well.</p>
<p>If frequent urination and unusual thirst continue without an obvious explanation, checking blood glucose may be appropriate.</p>
<h3>Unexplained Weight Loss Needs Attention</h3>
<p>Unexpected weight loss should not always be considered a positive sign.</p>
<p>When the body cannot use glucose efficiently for energy, it may begin relying more heavily on stored fat and muscle. This can contribute to weight loss even when a person has not intentionally changed their diet or exercise routine.</p>
<p>Unintentional weight loss should be discussed with a doctor, particularly when it occurs along with increased thirst, urination, or fatigue.</p>
<h3>Fatigue May Have More Than One Cause</h3>
<p>Feeling tired after inadequate sleep is common. But persistent exhaustion despite sufficient rest can have several possible causes, including diabetes.</p>
<p>When glucose is not being utilized effectively, a person may experience reduced energy. Fatigue can also be related to anaemia, thyroid disorders, infections, sleep problems, stress, or nutritional deficiencies.</p>
<p>That is why persistent fatigue should be assessed in context.</p>
<h3>Who Should Get Their Blood Sugar Checked?</h3>
<p>People with certain risk factors may benefit from regular diabetes screening.</p>
<h3>These include individuals with:</h3>
<h3>* A family history of diabetes</h3>
<h3>* Excess body weight</h3>
<h3>* High blood pressure</h3>
<h3>* Abnormal cholesterol</h3>
<h3>* Limited physical activity</h3>
<h3>* Previous elevated blood glucose</h3>
<h3>* Certain pregnancy-related risk factors</h3>
<p>The appropriate testing schedule depends on individual circumstances.</p>
<h3>How Is Diabetes Confirmed?</h3>
<p>Blood tests are used to evaluate glucose levels. Depending on the situation, testing may include fasting blood glucose, HbA1c, or other appropriate investigations.</p>
<p>HbA1c is useful because it provides an indication of average blood glucose over the previous few months rather than reflecting only one moment.</p>
<h3>What Can Help Reduce Diabetes Risk?</h3>
<p>Healthy habits can support better metabolic health.</p>
<h3>Focus on:</h3>
<h3>* Regular physical activity</h3>
<h3>* Balanced meals</h3>
<h3>* Adequate fibre intake</h3>
<h3>* Healthy weight management</h3>
<h3>* Good-quality sleep</h3>
<h3>* Stress management</h3>
<h3>* Avoiding tobacco</h3>
<h3>* Regular health assessments</h3>
<p>People already diagnosed with diabetes should follow an individualized treatment plan rather than making major medication changes independently.</p>
<h3>Conclusion</h3>
<p>Diabetes does not always announce itself with obvious symptoms. Changes such as excessive thirst, frequent urination, unexplained weight loss, persistent tiredness, blurred vision, or slow healing can be reasons to consider a health evaluation.</p>
<p>A simple blood test can provide valuable information about glucose levels. Identifying diabetes or prediabetes at an earlier stage can create an opportunity to manage the condition and reduce the likelihood of future complications.</p>
`
