    content: `
<p>Irregular periods are a common concern among women of different age groups. A menstrual cycle does not always follow the same pattern every month, and occasional changes may happen because of stress, changes in weight, travel, exercise, or other temporary factors. However, frequent or persistent changes in the menstrual cycle may sometimes indicate an underlying health condition that needs medical attention.</p>
<p>A menstrual cycle is generally considered regular when periods occur at fairly predictable intervals. The duration and flow can vary from woman to woman. Irregular periods may include cycles that are unusually short or long, missed periods, very heavy bleeding, or periods that occur unpredictably.</p>
<p>One of the common causes of irregular periods is hormonal imbalance. Conditions such as polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) can affect ovulation and lead to delayed, infrequent, or missed periods. Thyroid disorders may also influence menstrual patterns. Changes in reproductive hormones during puberty, after pregnancy, while breastfeeding, and around perimenopause can naturally affect the cycle.</p>
<p>Lifestyle factors can also contribute. Significant weight gain or weight loss, excessive physical activity, inadequate nutrition, poor sleep, and prolonged stress may interfere with normal hormonal function. Certain medications and underlying medical conditions may also affect menstruation.</p>
<p>Women should pay attention to changes in their usual menstrual pattern. Symptoms such as extremely heavy bleeding, severe abdominal or pelvic pain, bleeding between periods, bleeding after sexual intercourse, or repeatedly missed periods should not be ignored. If irregular periods continue for several months, consulting a qualified gynaecologist can help identify the possible cause.</p>
<p>During a consultation, the doctor may ask about menstrual history, lifestyle, medications, pregnancy possibility, previous medical conditions, and associated symptoms. Depending on the individual situation, investigations such as blood tests or pelvic ultrasound may be recommended. These tests can help evaluate hormone levels, thyroid function, ovarian health, and other possible causes.</p>
<p>Treatment depends entirely on the underlying reason. Some women may only require lifestyle modifications and observation, while others may need medication or specific treatment for conditions such as PCOS or thyroid disorders. Maintaining a healthy weight, eating a balanced diet, getting adequate sleep, managing stress, and staying physically active can support overall reproductive health.</p>
<p>Tracking periods using a calendar or mobile application can also be helpful. Recording the start date, duration, flow, pain, and other symptoms gives both the woman and her doctor a clearer picture of menstrual patterns.</p>
<p>For women experiencing persistent menstrual changes, professional evaluation can provide clarity instead of relying on assumptions or self-medication. Dr. Sabbu Likhitha, MBBS, MS (OBGY), DNB, Consultant Obstetrician and Gynaecologist, can provide appropriate evaluation and guidance based on individual health needs.</p>
<p>Irregular periods do not always indicate a serious problem, but they are worth discussing when they become persistent, unusual, or associated with other symptoms. Understanding your menstrual cycle and recognizing changes early can help support better reproductive and overall health.</p>
`
