    image: '/images/doctor-blogs/sabbu-likhitha-blog-4.jpg',
    content: `
<p>Menstrual bleeding can vary from woman to woman, but unusually heavy or prolonged periods may affect daily activities and overall health. Heavy menstrual bleeding is not simply an inconvenience. When it occurs repeatedly, it can lead to tiredness, weakness, anemia, and reduced quality of life.</p>
<p>Some women may notice that they need to change sanitary products very frequently or that bleeding lasts longer than usual. Passing large blood clots, experiencing bleeding that interferes with work or sleep, or feeling unusually tired during or after periods may also indicate that menstrual blood loss is excessive.</p>
<p>There are several possible causes of heavy menstrual bleeding. Hormonal imbalance is one common reason, particularly when ovulation is irregular. Conditions such as PCOS can sometimes contribute to abnormal bleeding patterns.</p>
<p>Structural problems involving the uterus may also cause heavy periods. Fibroids, which are non-cancerous growths in the uterine muscle, can increase menstrual bleeding in some women. Uterine polyps and adenomyosis are other possible causes. Certain bleeding disorders, thyroid problems, medications, and pregnancy-related conditions may also result in abnormal bleeding.</p>
<p>Age can influence the causes of heavy bleeding. Teenagers may experience irregular cycles while the reproductive system is still maturing, whereas women approaching menopause may experience hormonal fluctuations that affect menstrual patterns.</p>
<p>Persistent heavy bleeding can result in iron deficiency and anemia. Symptoms may include fatigue, weakness, dizziness, headaches, breathlessness, or reduced ability to perform everyday activities. Women experiencing these symptoms should seek medical evaluation rather than assuming that tiredness is simply part of having a period.</p>
<p>A medical consultation usually begins with a detailed discussion about menstrual history. The doctor may ask about cycle length, duration, bleeding volume, pain, clots, medications, pregnancy history, and other symptoms. Blood tests may be recommended to check hemoglobin and iron status, along with other investigations where appropriate.</p>
<p>An ultrasound may be advised to examine the uterus and ovaries when structural causes are suspected. Further tests may be considered depending on age, symptoms, medical history, and examination findings.</p>
<p>Treatment depends on the underlying cause and the woman's individual needs. Some women may benefit from medications that reduce bleeding or regulate hormonal patterns. Iron supplementation may be recommended when iron deficiency or anemia is present. When fibroids, polyps, adenomyosis, or another structural condition is responsible, additional treatment options may be discussed.</p>
<p>Women should not self-medicate with hormonal medicines or other treatments simply because someone they know experienced similar symptoms. The same symptom can have different causes in different women.</p>
<p>Dr. Sabbu Likhitha, MBBS, MS (OBGY), DNB, Consultant Obstetrician and Gynaecologist, can evaluate abnormal menstrual bleeding and guide patients toward appropriate investigations and treatment based on their individual condition.</p>
<p>Heavy periods should not be considered something women simply have to tolerate. Recognizing persistent changes and seeking timely medical advice can help identify the underlying cause, address anemia when present, and improve quality of life. Keeping a record of menstrual dates, bleeding patterns, pain, and associated symptoms can also help during a medical consultation.</p>
`
