    content: `
<p>ACL Tear Symptoms and Grades: Understanding the Severity of a Knee Ligament Injury</p>
<p>An anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) tear is one of the most common and important knee ligament injuries, particularly among people who participate in sports involving sudden changes in direction, jumping, pivoting or rapid stopping. However, an ACL injury can also occur during a fall, road accident or everyday activity.</p>
<p>The ACL is one of the major ligaments inside the knee. It helps control forward movement of the shin bone and contributes to the stability of the knee during walking, running, jumping and turning.</p>
<p>An ACL injury can range from a mild stretch to a complete rupture. Understanding the symptoms and grades of an ACL tear can help patients seek appropriate medical evaluation and treatment at the right time.</p>
<h3>What Is an ACL Tear?</h3>
<p>The anterior cruciate ligament connects the thigh bone (femur) to the shin bone (tibia). Together with other ligaments and structures in the knee, it helps maintain stability during movement.</p>
<p>An ACL injury may occur when the knee suddenly twists or changes direction while the foot remains planted. Common situations include football, cricket, basketball, badminton, tennis, skiing and other sports. A direct blow to the knee or a road traffic accident can also cause an ACL injury.</p>
<p>Not every ACL injury is a complete tear. Doctors generally classify ligament injuries according to their severity.</p>
<h3>Common Symptoms of an ACL Tear</h3>
<p>Symptoms can vary depending on whether the ligament has been stretched, partially torn or completely ruptured.</p>
<h3>1. A “Popping” Sensation</h3>
<p>Some people experience or hear a sudden “pop” at the time of injury. This can occur when the ACL is injured, although not everyone with an ACL tear experiences a popping sensation.</p>
<h3>2. Sudden Knee Pain</h3>
<p>Pain may develop immediately after the injury. The intensity can vary depending on the extent of ligament damage and whether other structures, such as the meniscus or cartilage, are also injured.</p>
<h3>3. Rapid Swelling</h3>
<p>Swelling within the first few hours after a knee injury can be an important sign. Bleeding into the knee joint, known as a haemarthrosis, can occur following certain ACL injuries.</p>
<h3>4. Difficulty Putting Weight on the Leg</h3>
<p>Some patients may find it difficult or painful to stand or walk after an ACL injury. Others may be able to walk but experience instability or discomfort.</p>
<h3>5. Knee Instability or “Giving Way”</h3>
<p>A feeling that the knee is giving way, buckling or shifting is a common concern after an ACL injury. This may become particularly noticeable when turning, changing direction or walking on uneven ground.</p>
<h3>6. Reduced Range of Motion</h3>
<p>Swelling and pain can make it difficult to fully bend or straighten the knee.</p>
<h3>7. Difficulty During Sports or Pivoting Movements</h3>
<p>Patients with ACL injuries may have difficulty with running, jumping, sudden direction changes or other movements that require knee stability.</p>
<p>It is important to remember that symptoms alone cannot confirm an ACL tear. A clinical examination and appropriate imaging, such as an MRI when indicated, may be required to assess the injury.</p>
<h3>What Are the Grades of an ACL Injury?</h3>
<p>ACL injuries are commonly described in three grades based on the extent of ligament damage.</p>
<p>Grade 1 ACL Injury — Mild Sprain</p>
<p>In a Grade 1 injury, the ACL has been stretched but remains largely intact.</p>
<p>Typical features may include:</p>
<ul><li>Mild pain or tenderness</li>
<li>Limited swelling</li>
<li>Minimal instability</li>
<li>The ligament continues to provide knee stability</li></ul>
<p>Many Grade 1 ligament injuries can be managed without surgery. Treatment may include rest, activity modification, pain management when appropriate and a structured physiotherapy programme.</p>
<p>The exact treatment depends on the patient's symptoms and clinical examination.</p>
<p>Grade 2 ACL Injury — Partial Tear</p>
<p>A Grade 2 ACL injury involves partial damage to the ligament. Some ligament fibres are torn, while some remain intact.</p>
<p>Patients may experience:</p>
<ul><li>More noticeable pain</li>
<li>Swelling</li>
<li>Reduced knee movement</li>
<li>Difficulty with sports activities</li>
<li>Some degree of instability</li></ul>
<p>Treatment for a partial ACL tear depends on factors such as the extent and location of the tear, knee stability, associated injuries, activity level and treatment goals.</p>
<p>Some partial ACL injuries can be managed with rehabilitation, while others may require further specialist assessment and, in selected cases, surgery.</p>
<p>Grade 3 ACL Injury — Complete Tear</p>
<p>A Grade 3 injury generally refers to a complete rupture of the ACL.</p>
<p>The ligament is no longer able to provide normal stability to the knee.</p>
<p>Possible symptoms include:</p>
<ul><li>Significant swelling</li>
<li>Pain following the injury</li>
<li>Knee instability</li>
<li>Repeated giving-way episodes</li>
<li>Difficulty with pivoting and sports</li>
<li>Reduced confidence while using the injured leg</li></ul>
<p>A complete ACL tear does not automatically mean every patient requires surgery. Treatment decisions depend on several factors, including age, activity level, symptoms, knee stability, associated injuries and the patient's functional requirements.</p>
<p>For individuals who participate in pivoting sports or have persistent instability, an orthopaedic evaluation is particularly important.</p>
<h3>Can an ACL Tear Heal on Its Own?</h3>
<p>The answer depends on the type and extent of the injury.</p>
<p>Some mild or partial ligament injuries may improve with appropriate rehabilitation. A completely ruptured ACL has limited capacity for spontaneous healing compared with some other ligament injuries.</p>
<p>However, treatment should not be decided based on the MRI report alone.</p>
<p>An orthopaedic surgeon may evaluate:</p>
<ul><li>The patient's symptoms</li>
<li>Physical examination findings</li>
<li>Knee stability</li>
<li>MRI findings</li>
<li>Associated meniscus or cartilage injuries</li>
<li>Age and activity level</li>
<li>Sports and occupational requirements</li>
<li>Previous knee injuries</li></ul>
<p>Based on these factors, the treatment plan may involve physiotherapy, activity modification, bracing in selected cases, or ACL reconstruction when clinically appropriate.</p>
<h3>When Should You See an Orthopaedic Surgeon?</h3>
<p>Seek medical evaluation if you experience significant swelling after a knee injury, persistent pain, difficulty walking, repeated knee instability or a sensation that the knee is giving way.</p>
<p>Early assessment can help identify an ACL injury and associated problems such as meniscus, cartilage or other ligament injuries.</p>
<h3>Final Takeaway</h3>
<p>An ACL tear can range from a mild Grade 1 sprain to a complete Grade 3 rupture. Symptoms such as sudden pain, swelling, a popping sensation, reduced movement and knee instability can indicate an ACL injury, but they are not enough to determine its severity by themselves.</p>
<p>Early diagnosis and an appropriate rehabilitation or surgical plan can help patients work toward restoring knee stability and returning to their desired activities.</p>
<p>If you have suffered a knee injury and suspect an ACL tear, consult an experienced orthopaedic and arthroscopy surgeon for a proper clinical assessment and personalised treatment plan.</p>
    `
