    content: `
<p>5 Signs Your Knee Arthritis May Be Getting Worse</p>
<p>Knee arthritis is a common cause of persistent knee pain, stiffness and reduced mobility. In the early stages, symptoms may be occasional and manageable. Over time, however, arthritis can become more troublesome and begin interfering with everyday activities.</p>
<p>Recognising changes in your symptoms can help you seek an orthopaedic evaluation at the right time. Here are five signs that your knee arthritis may be progressing.</p>
<h3>1. Your Knee Pain Is Becoming More Frequent</h3>
<p>One of the most noticeable signs is a change from occasional pain to more frequent or persistent discomfort.</p>
<p>Initially, you may experience pain only after prolonged walking, exercise or climbing stairs. As symptoms progress, pain may occur during routine activities such as standing, walking short distances or getting up from a chair.</p>
<p>Persistent pain does not necessarily mean that knee replacement is required, but it is a reason to have your knee evaluated.</p>
<h3>2. Everyday Activities Are Becoming Difficult</h3>
<p>Knee arthritis can gradually affect activities that were previously easy.</p>
<p>You may notice difficulty with:</p>
<ul><li>Climbing or descending stairs</li>
<li>Walking for longer distances</li>
<li>Standing for extended periods</li>
<li>Getting up from a chair</li>
<li>Getting in and out of a vehicle</li>
<li>Squatting or bending the knee</li></ul>
<p>If knee pain is increasingly affecting your work, sleep, exercise or daily routine, it may indicate that your condition requires reassessment.</p>
<h3>3. Knee Stiffness Is Increasing</h3>
<p>Morning stiffness or stiffness after sitting for a long time can occur with knee arthritis.</p>
<p>Some people also notice that the knee takes longer to “loosen up” after inactivity.</p>
<p>Increasing stiffness may restrict the range of motion and make movements such as bending or straightening the knee more difficult.</p>
<p>Regular appropriate exercise and physiotherapy may help some patients maintain mobility, but persistent or worsening stiffness should be discussed with an orthopaedic specialist.</p>
<h3>4. Your Knee Is Swelling or Feeling Unstable</h3>
<p>Arthritis can sometimes be associated with swelling around the knee joint.</p>
<p>You may notice that your knee feels swollen after walking or physical activity. Some patients may also experience sensations of weakness or instability.</p>
<p>However, repeated giving-way episodes can also occur because of ligament or other knee problems. Therefore, recurring instability should not automatically be attributed to arthritis.</p>
<p>An orthopaedic assessment can help identify the underlying cause.</p>
<h3>5. Pain Is Affecting Your Sleep</h3>
<p>When knee pain begins disturbing your sleep, it can have a significant impact on quality of life.</p>
<p>Some patients may find it difficult to get comfortable in bed or may wake because of knee discomfort.</p>
<p>Night pain can have several causes, so persistent symptoms should be evaluated rather than managed only with pain medication.</p>
<h3>Does Worsening Arthritis Always Mean Knee Replacement?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily.</p>
<p>Treatment depends on the severity of arthritis, symptoms, physical examination, imaging findings and how much the condition affects your daily life.</p>
<p>Depending on the individual situation, treatment may include:</p>
<ul><li>Exercise and physiotherapy</li>
<li>Weight management</li>
<li>Activity modification</li>
<li>Medications when appropriate</li>
<li>Injections in selected patients</li>
<li>Assistive devices</li>
<li>Surgical treatment when clinically indicated</li></ul>
<p>Knee replacement may be considered when advanced joint damage causes significant symptoms and non-surgical treatment no longer provides adequate relief.</p>
<h3>When Should You See an Orthopaedic Surgeon?</h3>
<p>Consider an evaluation if your knee pain is persistent, progressively worsening, affecting sleep or limiting normal activities.</p>
<p>Early assessment can help determine the severity of arthritis and identify appropriate treatment options.</p>
<h3>Final Takeaway</h3>
<p>Increasing pain, worsening stiffness, difficulty with daily activities, swelling or instability, and sleep disturbance can all be signs that your knee arthritis needs reassessment.</p>
<p>Do not assume that worsening knee pain is simply a normal part of ageing. A timely orthopaedic evaluation can help you understand what is happening inside your knee and choose an appropriate treatment plan.</p>
    `
