    content: `
<h3>Why Does Knee Pain Get Worse After Sitting for a Long Time?</h3>
<h3>Have you ever sat for an hour and noticed that your knees hurt or feel stiff when you finally stand up?</h3>
<p>This is a common complaint, particularly among people with knee arthritis and other joint conditions. Some patients describe it as a “stiff knee,” while others experience pain during the first few steps after getting up.</p>
<h3>But why does this happen?</h3>
<h3>What Happens to the Knee During Prolonged Sitting?</h3>
<p>When you sit for a long period, your knee remains in a relatively fixed position. The muscles around the joint are less active, and the knee is not moving through its normal range of motion.</p>
<p>People with an already painful or arthritic knee may notice stiffness more strongly after this period of inactivity.</p>
<p>This phenomenon is sometimes described as “start-up pain”—discomfort or stiffness that occurs when beginning to move after resting.</p>
<h3>Knee Arthritis Can Be One Possible Cause</h3>
<p>Osteoarthritis occurs when the tissues within the joint undergo degenerative changes. It can cause pain, stiffness and reduced mobility.</p>
<p>People with knee arthritis may notice symptoms particularly after:</p>
<ul><li>Sitting for a long time</li>
<li>Getting out of bed</li>
<li>Travelling by car</li>
<li>Sitting through a movie</li>
<li>Working at a desk</li>
<li>Travelling by flight or bus</li></ul>
<p>The first few steps may be uncomfortable, followed by some improvement as the knee begins moving.</p>
<h3>Why Does Movement Sometimes Help?</h3>
<p>Gentle movement can help the knee gradually return to activity after prolonged rest.</p>
<p>Simple movements such as slowly straightening and bending the knee, standing periodically and taking short walks may help reduce stiffness for some people.</p>
<p>However, exercise should be appropriate for your condition. If movement consistently causes significant pain or swelling, consult a healthcare professional before increasing activity.</p>
<h3>It May Not Always Be Arthritis</h3>
<p>Knee pain after sitting can have several possible causes.</p>
<p>These may include:</p>
<ul><li>Osteoarthritis</li>
<li>Patellofemoral pain</li>
<li>Previous knee injury</li>
<li>Meniscus problems</li>
<li>Tendon-related conditions</li>
<li>Muscle weakness</li>
<li>Inflammation around the joint</li></ul>
<p>Therefore, persistent knee pain should not automatically be diagnosed as arthritis without an appropriate clinical evaluation.</p>
<h3>When Should You Be Concerned?</h3>
<p>Consider seeing an orthopaedic specialist if you experience:</p>
<ul><li>Persistent or worsening knee pain</li>
<li>Significant swelling</li>
<li>Difficulty walking</li>
<li>Repeated knee locking</li>
<li>Knee instability or giving way</li>
<li>Reduced ability to bend or straighten the knee</li>
<li>Pain that interferes with sleep or daily activities</li></ul>
<p>A clinical examination can help determine the likely cause and whether imaging is necessary.</p>
<h3>What Can You Do About Knee Stiffness?</h3>
<p>Depending on the underlying cause, your doctor may recommend measures such as:</p>
<ul><li>Regular low-impact physical activity</li>
<li>Strengthening exercises</li>
<li>Physiotherapy</li>
<li>Weight management when appropriate</li>
<li>Avoiding prolonged periods of inactivity</li>
<li>Medication when medically appropriate</li>
<li>Other treatments based on the diagnosis</li></ul>
<p>If you work at a desk, taking appropriate movement breaks can also help prevent your knee from remaining in one position for too long.</p>
<h3>Final Takeaway</h3>
<p>Knee pain and stiffness after sitting for a long time can occur for several reasons, with arthritis being one possible cause.</p>
<p>If your knees repeatedly hurt when you stand after prolonged sitting, especially if the symptoms are becoming more frequent or are affecting your daily activities, consider getting an orthopaedic evaluation.</p>
<p>Understanding the underlying cause is the first step toward choosing the right treatment.</p>
    `
