    content: `
<h3>Life After Knee Replacement: What Activities Can You Return To?</h3>
<p>One of the biggest questions patients ask before knee replacement surgery is:</p>
<p>“What will I be able to do after my knee replacement?”</p>
<p>Modern knee replacement is designed to reduce pain and improve function in people with significant knee joint damage. Recovery is gradual, and the activities a patient can safely return to depend on their health, surgical recovery, muscle strength, balance and guidance from their orthopaedic team.</p>
<p>The good news is that many patients are able to return to a wide range of everyday activities after successful rehabilitation.</p>
<h3>Walking After Knee Replacement</h3>
<p>Walking is an important part of recovery.</p>
<p>Patients are generally encouraged to begin appropriate movement early after surgery according to their surgeon's protocol. Walking distance and independence gradually increase as strength and confidence improve.</p>
<p>Initially, walking aids may be necessary. Their use can be reduced as recommended by the treating team.</p>
<p>The exact timeline varies from patient to patient.</p>
<h3>Climbing Stairs</h3>
<p>Many patients eventually return to using stairs.</p>
<p>During the early recovery period, stairs may be challenging because the muscles around the knee are still regaining strength.</p>
<p>Physiotherapy can help patients develop the strength, coordination and confidence needed for stair climbing.</p>
<p>Driving</p>
<p>Returning to driving depends on several factors, including which leg was operated on, pain control, reaction ability, mobility and the patient's ability to safely control the vehicle.</p>
<p>Your surgeon should advise you when it is appropriate to resume driving.</p>
<p>Patients should not drive while taking medications that impair alertness or when they cannot safely operate the vehicle.</p>
<p>Returning to Work</p>
<p>The time required to return to work depends largely on the type of job.</p>
<p>A person with a desk-based occupation may return sooner than someone whose work involves:</p>
<ul><li>Heavy lifting</li>
<li>Frequent squatting</li>
<li>Climbing</li>
<li>Prolonged standing</li>
<li>Physically demanding activity</li></ul>
<p>Your surgeon can provide individual guidance based on your recovery.</p>
<h3>Exercise After Knee Replacement</h3>
<p>Regular physical activity is important after recovery.</p>
<p>Many patients can participate in lower-impact activities such as:</p>
<ul><li>Walking</li>
<li>Stationary cycling</li>
<li>Swimming after the wound has healed and the surgeon permits it</li>
<li>Appropriate strengthening exercises</li>
<li>Certain low-impact fitness activities</li></ul>
<p>The goal is to maintain muscle strength, joint movement, balance and overall fitness.</p>
<h3>What About Squatting and Sitting Cross-Legged?</h3>
<p>These activities can be particularly important culturally and functionally for many patients.</p>
<p>The ability to squat deeply or sit cross-legged after knee replacement varies from person to person and may be affected by knee movement, muscle strength, surgical factors and individual anatomy.</p>
<p>Do not force deep bending during recovery. Discuss your specific goals with your surgeon and physiotherapist.</p>
<h3>Can You Play Sports?</h3>
<p>Patients may often return to selected recreational activities after adequate recovery and medical clearance.</p>
<p>Lower-impact activities are generally more suitable for a replaced joint than high-impact activities involving repeated jumping, running or sudden twisting.</p>
<p>Your surgeon can guide you regarding which activities are appropriate based on your individual circumstances.</p>
<h3>Why Is Physiotherapy Important?</h3>
<p>Surgery is only one part of the recovery process.</p>
<p>Physiotherapy can help with:</p>
<ul><li>Knee movement</li>
<li>Muscle strengthening</li>
<li>Walking ability</li>
<li>Balance</li>
<li>Functional activities</li>
<li>Gradual return to normal routines</li></ul>
<p>Following the rehabilitation programme consistently can be an important part of recovery.</p>
<h3>Does Knee Replacement Mean You Must Stop Being Active?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily.</p>
<p>The goal of knee replacement is to reduce pain and improve function, allowing patients to participate in appropriate daily and recreational activities.</p>
<p>However, protecting the artificial joint remains important. Patients should discuss high-impact activities with their surgeon before returning to them.</p>
<h3>Final Takeaway</h3>
<p>Life after knee replacement can include walking, travelling, working, exercising and many everyday activities.</p>
<p>Recovery is individual, and returning to activities should happen gradually rather than being based on a fixed timeline.</p>
<p>If you are considering knee replacement, discuss your personal goals with your orthopaedic surgeon before surgery. Knowing what activities matter most to you can help your surgical and rehabilitation team plan your recovery appropriately.</p>
    `
