    content: `
<h3>When Is Knee Pain Serious Enough to See an Orthopaedic Surgeon?</h3>
<p>Knee pain is extremely common. It can occur after exercise, an injury, prolonged activity or simply after spending a long time on your feet.</p>
<h3>But when should knee pain be taken seriously?</h3>
<p>Not every episode of knee discomfort requires a specialist consultation. However, persistent, worsening or function-limiting knee pain deserves medical attention.</p>
<p>Knowing the warning signs can help you avoid unnecessarily delaying diagnosis and treatment.</p>
<h3>1. Your Knee Pain Is Persistent</h3>
<p>If knee pain continues for weeks or repeatedly returns, it is worth understanding the underlying cause.</p>
<p>Persistent pain may be associated with conditions such as osteoarthritis, meniscus injury, ligament problems, tendon disorders or previous trauma.</p>
<p>Rather than repeatedly treating the symptom without knowing the cause, an orthopaedic evaluation can help determine what is happening.</p>
<h3>2. Pain Is Affecting Everyday Activities</h3>
<p>Knee pain becomes more concerning when it starts interfering with normal life.</p>
<p>For example, you may struggle with:</p>
<ul><li>Walking</li>
<li>Climbing stairs</li>
<li>Getting up from a chair</li>
<li>Standing for long periods</li>
<li>Getting into a vehicle</li>
<li>Exercising</li>
<li>Working</li>
<li>Sleeping</li></ul>
<p>If these limitations are becoming progressively worse, consider consulting an orthopaedic specialist.</p>
<h3>3. Your Knee Is Swollen</h3>
<p>Swelling can occur after an injury or due to inflammation and other joint conditions.</p>
<p>Sudden significant swelling after a twisting injury may require prompt assessment, particularly when accompanied by pain, instability or difficulty bearing weight.</p>
<p>Persistent swelling should also be evaluated to determine its cause.</p>
<h3>4. Your Knee Is Giving Way or Locking</h3>
<p>A knee that repeatedly feels as though it is giving way, buckling or collapsing should not be ignored.</p>
<p>Similarly, a knee that becomes mechanically locked or repeatedly catches during movement may indicate an internal knee problem.</p>
<p>These symptoms can occur with ligament or meniscus injuries and require clinical assessment.</p>
<h3>5. You Heard a Pop During an Injury</h3>
<p>A sudden popping sensation during sports, a fall or a twisting movement can sometimes occur with ligament injuries such as an ACL tear.</p>
<p>If the popping sensation is followed by swelling, pain or instability, an orthopaedic evaluation is advisable.</p>
<h3>6. You Cannot Fully Bend or Straighten Your Knee</h3>
<p>A significant reduction in knee movement may result from pain, swelling, arthritis, injury or other structural problems.</p>
<p>If your knee progressively becomes harder to bend or straighten, consult a specialist rather than repeatedly forcing the joint.</p>
<h3>7. Pain Is Disturbing Your Sleep</h3>
<p>Occasional discomfort is different from persistent pain that repeatedly wakes you at night or prevents you from getting comfortable.</p>
<p>Night pain can have several possible causes and should be discussed with a doctor when it persists.</p>
<h3>When Should You Seek Urgent Medical Attention?</h3>
<p>Some knee problems require more immediate medical evaluation.</p>
<p>Seek prompt medical care if knee pain follows significant trauma and you cannot bear weight, if there is obvious deformity, rapidly increasing swelling, severe pain, or signs of infection such as fever with a hot, red and swollen joint.</p>
<h3>What Happens During an Orthopaedic Consultation?</h3>
<p>An orthopaedic evaluation generally begins with a discussion of your symptoms and medical history, followed by a physical examination.</p>
<p>Depending on the suspected condition, your doctor may recommend imaging such as an X-ray or MRI.</p>
<p>Treatment depends on the diagnosis.</p>
<p>Possible approaches may include:</p>
<ul><li>Activity modification</li>
<li>Physiotherapy</li>
<li>Exercise programmes</li>
<li>Medication when appropriate</li>
<li>Injections in selected cases</li>
<li>Arthroscopic procedures</li>
<li>Joint replacement when clinically indicated</li></ul>
<p>Not every patient with knee pain requires surgery.</p>
<h3>Final Takeaway</h3>
<p>Knee pain should not automatically be considered a normal part of ageing.</p>
<p>Persistent pain, swelling, instability, locking, reduced movement, difficulty walking or symptoms following an injury are reasons to seek an orthopaedic evaluation.</p>
<p>Early diagnosis can help identify the cause of your knee pain and determine whether you need conservative treatment, rehabilitation, arthroscopy, joint replacement or another appropriate approach.</p>
<p>If knee pain is interfering with your everyday life, don't simply learn to live with it. A proper orthopaedic assessment can help you understand your options and make an informed treatment decision.</p>
    `
