    content: `
<h3>What Does 70% Heart Blockage Actually Mean?</h3>
<p>Hearing the words “70% heart blockage” can be frightening. Many patients immediately assume that the artery is 70% closed and that an emergency procedure is automatically required.</p>
<p>However, the percentage of blockage is only one part of the overall clinical picture. Understanding what the number means—and what it does not mean—can help patients have a more informed conversation with their cardiologist.</p>
<h3>What Is a 70% Coronary Blockage?</h3>
<p>A coronary artery carries oxygen-rich blood to the heart muscle. When plaque develops inside the artery, the available space for blood flow can become narrower.</p>
<p>When doctors describe a lesion as approximately 70% narrowed, they are generally referring to the estimated reduction in the diameter of a particular segment of the artery compared with a reference segment.</p>
<p>It does not mean that exactly 70% of the entire artery is filled with cholesterol.</p>
<h3>Does 70% Automatically Mean You Need a Stent?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily.</p>
<p>Treatment decisions depend on several factors, including:</p>
<ul><li>Which coronary artery is affected</li>
<li>The exact location of the narrowing</li>
<li>Whether there are multiple blockages</li>
<li>The patient's symptoms</li>
<li>Evidence of reduced blood flow to the heart</li>
<li>Heart function</li>
<li>Other medical conditions</li>
<li>Overall coronary anatomy</li></ul>
<p>In some situations, further testing may be used to determine whether a particular narrowing is actually limiting blood flow.</p>
<h3>Why Is the Location Important?</h3>
<p>A narrowing in a major coronary vessel may have different implications from a narrowing in a smaller branch.</p>
<p>Doctors therefore consider the anatomy rather than looking at the percentage alone.</p>
<p>For example, a patient with one lesion and a patient with several complex lesions may both be told they have a “70% blockage,” but their treatment plans may be very different.</p>
<h3>What Tests Can Help?</h3>
<p>Depending on the clinical situation, cardiologists may use anatomical and functional assessments.</p>
<p>These can include:</p>
<ul><li>ECG</li>
<li>Echocardiogram</li>
<li>Stress testing in selected patients</li>
<li>CT coronary angiography</li>
<li>Invasive coronary angiography</li>
<li>Physiological measurements such as FFR or iFR in appropriate cases</li></ul>
<p>These tests can help determine whether a narrowing is clinically significant and guide treatment decisions.</p>
<h3>Can a 70% Blockage Cause Symptoms?</h3>
<p>It can, particularly when the narrowing limits blood flow to the heart muscle during increased demand.</p>
<p>Symptoms may include chest pressure or discomfort, breathlessness, reduced exercise tolerance or unusual fatigue.</p>
<p>However, symptoms vary significantly between individuals. Some people may have significant coronary disease with few or no obvious symptoms.</p>
<h3>Is a 70% Blockage an Emergency?</h3>
<p>The percentage alone does not determine whether the situation is an emergency.</p>
<p>A person with stable symptoms and a known coronary narrowing may require planned evaluation and treatment. In contrast, someone with sudden chest pressure, sweating, breathlessness or other symptoms of an acute coronary syndrome may require immediate emergency care.</p>
<p>The clinical context is therefore extremely important.</p>
<h3>What Happens After a Blockage Is Diagnosed?</h3>
<p>Depending on the patient's condition, treatment may involve lifestyle changes, medications, revascularization with angioplasty and stenting, or in some cases referral for bypass surgery.</p>
<p>The objective is not simply to “remove a percentage of blockage.” Treatment is designed to reduce symptoms, improve blood flow when appropriate and reduce cardiovascular risk.</p>
<h3>The Takeaway</h3>
<p>A “70% blockage” is a description of coronary artery narrowing, not a complete diagnosis or automatic instruction for a stent.</p>
<p>The significance depends on the artery involved, the location and characteristics of the lesion, symptoms, blood-flow assessment and the patient's overall cardiovascular condition.</p>
<h3>If you have been told you have a 70% blockage, ask your cardiologist: Which artery is affected? Is the narrowing causing reduced blood flow? What treatment options are appropriate for my situation?</h3>
<p>Understanding the complete picture is more useful than focusing on the percentage alone.</p>
    `
